window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-17"]=[{"id":"q2-270-271","q":"2:270–271","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:270–271 · Vows, open giving and hidden charity","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 304–305","cairo":"I:304–305","source_page_first":304,"source_page_last":305,"ar":"وَما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ نَفَقَةٍ أَوْ نَذَرْتُمْ مِنْ نَذْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُهُ وَما لِلظَّالِمِينَ مِنْ أَنْصارٍ (270) إِنْ تُبْدُوا الصَّدَقاتِ فَنِعِمَّا هِيَ وَإِنْ تُخْفُوها وَتُؤْتُوهَا الْفُقَراءَ فَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَيُكَفِّرُ عَنْكُمْ مِنْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ (271)\nقلت: النذْر: هو إلزَامُ المكلف نفسه ما لم يَجب، كقوله: لله عليَّ أن أتصدق بكذا، أو أصلي كذا، أو أن أصوم كذا، أو إن شفى الله مريضي فعليَّ كذا، فمن نطق بشيء من ذلك لزمه، ومن علق بشيء وحصل ذلك لزمه ما نطق به. و (نعما) أصلها: نِعم ما هي، فأدغمت الميم في الميم، وفي (نعم) : ثلاث لغات: «نعم» بفتح النون وكسر العين وهي الأصل، وبسكونها، وبكسر النون وسكون العين، فمن قرأ بكسر النون والعين، فعلى لغة كسر العين، وأتبع النون للعين، ومن اختلس، أشار إلى لغة السكون، ومن قرأ بفتح النون وكسر العين، فعلى الأصل وأدغم المثلين، ومن قرأ بفتح النون وسكون العين فعلى لغة (نعم) بالفتح والسكون، ثم أدغم، ولم يعتبر التقاء الساكنين لعروضه، أو لكون الثاني مُشدَّداً سهل ذلك. والله أعلم.\nومن قرأ: (ونكَّفرْ) ، بالجزم، فعطف على محل الجزاء، ومن قرأ بالرفع، فعلى الاستئناف، أي: ونحن نكفر، أو: فهو يكفر، على القراءتين.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ نَفَقَةٍ قليلة أو كثيرة، سرّاً أو علانية، في حق أو باطل، أَوْ نَذَرْتُمْ مِنْ نَذْرٍ بشرط أو بغير شرط، في طاعة أو معصية، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُهُ، فيجازيكم عليه، فمن أنفق في طاعة أو نذر قربة كان من المحسنين، ومن أنفق في معصية أو نذر معصية كان من الظالمين. وَما لِلظَّالِمِينَ مِنْ أَنْصارٍ ينصرونهم من عذاب الله.\nإِنْ تُظهروا الصَّدَقاتِ، مخلصين فيها، فَنِعِمَّا هِيَ أي: فنعم شيئاً إبداؤها، ولا سيما للمقتدى به، فهو أفضل في حقه، وَإِنْ تُخْفُوها وَتُؤْتُوهَا الْفُقَراءَ خفية فَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ لأنه أقرب للإخلاص، وهذا\nفي التطوع، تفضل عَلانيتها بسبعين ضعفاً. وأما الفريضة ففيها تفصيل، فمن خاف على نفسه شوْبَ الرياء أخفى أو نَوّب، ومَنْ أَمِنَ أَظْهَر. فقد ورد أن علانية الفريضة تَفْضُلُ سرَّها بخمسة وعشرين ضعفاً، فإن فعلتم ما أُمرتم به في الوجهين، فقد أحسنتم، ونكفر عَنْكُمْ مِنْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ أي: نستر عنكم بعض ذنوبكم، وقد ورد في صدقة السر أن صاحبها يُظله الله يوم لا ظِلَّ إِلا ظله وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ لا يخفى عليه مَن أَسَرَّ أو جَهَر، ومن أخلص أو خلط، ففيه ترغيب وترهيب. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: معاملة العبد مع مولاه: إما أن تكون لطلب الأجور، وإما لرفع الستور، فالأول يُعطي أجره من وراء الباب، والثاني يدخل مع الأحباب. وأما العامل للدنيا فهو ظالم لنفسه (وما للظالمين من أنصار) ، وفي بعض الآثار: طالب الدنيا أسير، وطالب الآخرة أجير، وطالب الحق أمير.\nثُم الناس في معاملة الحق على أقسام ثلاثة: قِسْمٍ يليق بهم الإخفاء والإسرار، وهم طالبو الإخلاص من المريدين السائرين. وقسْمٍ يليق بهم الإظهار وهم أهل الاقتداء من العلماء المخلصين. وقسْم لا يقفون مع ظهور ولا خفاء، بل مع ما يبرز في الوقت، وهم العارفون الكاملون. ولذلك قال الشيخ أبو العباس رضي الله عنه: (مَن أحبَّ الظهور فهو عبد الظهور، ومَن أحبّ الخفاء فهو عبد الخفاء، ومن كان عبد الله فسواء عليه أظهره أم أخفاه) .\nوالهداية كلها بيد الله، ليس لغيره منها شىء، كما أبان ذلك الحقّ جلّ جلاله بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ نَفَقَةٍ أَوْ نَذَرْتُمْ مِنْ نَذْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُهُ وَما لِلظَّالِمِينَ مِنْ أَنْصارٍ (270) إِنْ تُبْدُوا الصَّدَقاتِ فَنِعِمَّا هِيَ وَإِنْ تُخْفُوها وَتُؤْتُوهَا الْفُقَراءَ فَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَيُكَفِّرُ عَنْكُمْ مِنْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ (271)","quran_en":"270. Whatever expenditure you make or vow you undertake, God knows it. The wrongdoers have no helpers. 271. If you disclose your charities, how excellent they are! If you conceal them and give them to the poor, that is better for you, and He will efface some of your evil deeds. God is aware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Vows and the readings of niʿimmā","html":"<p>I say: a vow (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النذر<\/bdi>) is a legally responsible person’s imposing upon himself something not already obligatory: “For God, I must give such-and-such in charity,” “pray such-and-such,” “fast such-and-such,” or “If God heals my sick person, such-and-such is due from me.” Whoever utters one of these is bound by it; whoever makes it conditional is bound when the condition occurs.<\/p><p><i>Niʿimmā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعمّا<\/bdi>) is originally <i>niʿma mā hiya<\/i>, with one <i>mīm<\/i> assimilated into the other. The verb has three forms: <i>naʿima<\/i>, with <i>fatḥa<\/i> on the <i>nūn<\/i> and <i>kasra<\/i> on the <i>ʿayn<\/i>, the original form; <i>naʿma<\/i>, with quiescent <i>ʿayn<\/i>; and <i>niʿma<\/i>, with <i>kasra<\/i> on the <i>nūn<\/i> and quiescent <i>ʿayn<\/i>.<\/p><p>The reading with <i>kasra<\/i> on both consonants follows the form with <i>kasra<\/i> on the <i>ʿayn<\/i>, making the <i>nūn<\/i> conform to it. A reduced vowel (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اختلاس<\/bdi>) points towards the form with quiescence. The reading with <i>fatḥa<\/i> on the <i>nūn<\/i> and <i>kasra<\/i> on the <i>ʿayn<\/i> retains the original and assimilates the two identical consonants. The reading with <i>fatḥa<\/i> on the <i>nūn<\/i> and quiescent <i>ʿayn<\/i> follows <i>naʿma<\/i> and then assimilates, disregarding the meeting of two quiescent consonants because it is incidental, or because the second is doubled, making it easier. And God knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-255\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B227]<\/a><\/p><p>The jussive reading of “We efface” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نكفّرْ<\/bdi>) is coordinated with the syntactic position of the conditional consequence. The indicative reading begins a new statement: “We efface,” or “He effaces,” according to the two pronominal readings.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: a vow ( النذر ) is a legally responsible person’s imposing upon himself something not already obligatory: “For God, I must give such-and-such in charity,” “pray such-and-such,” “fast such-and-such,” or “If God heals my sick person, such-and-such is due from me.” Whoever utters one of these is bound by it; whoever makes it conditional is bound when the condition occurs. Niʿimmā ( نعمّا ) is originally niʿma mā hiya , with one mīm assimilated into the other. The verb has three forms: naʿima , with fatḥa on the nūn and kasra on the ʿayn , the original form; naʿma , with quiescent ʿayn ; and niʿma , with kasra on the nūn and quiescent ʿayn . The reading with kasra on both consonants follows the form with kasra on the ʿayn , making the nūn conform to it. A reduced vowel ( اختلاس ) points towards the form with quiescence. The reading with fatḥa on the nūn and kasra on the ʿayn retains the original and assimilates the two identical consonants. The reading with fatḥa on the nūn and quiescent ʿayn follows naʿma and then assimilates, disregarding the meeting of two quiescent consonants because it is incidental, or because the second is doubled, making it easier. And God knows best. [B227] The jussive reading of “We efface” ( نكفّرْ ) is coordinated with the syntactic position of the conditional consequence. The indicative reading begins a new statement: “We efface,” or “He effaces,” according to the two pronominal readings."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The intention and manner of expenditure","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: whatever you spend, little or much, secretly or openly, rightly or wrongly; or whatever vow you undertake, conditionally or unconditionally, in obedience or disobedience—God knows it and will recompense you. Whoever spends in obedience or vows a pious act is among those who do good. Whoever spends in disobedience or vows disobedience is among the wrongdoers, who have no helpers to save them from God’s punishment.<\/p><p>If you disclose your charities sincerely, how excellent their disclosure is—especially for someone whose example is followed, for whom it is preferable. If you conceal them and give to the poor secretly, that is better, being nearer to sincerity. This concerns<\/p><p>voluntary charity: its secrecy surpasses its publicity seventyfold. Obligatory giving requires a distinction. Someone fearing an admixture of ostentation should conceal it or act through a deputy; someone secure from that should make it public. It is transmitted that the publicity of an obligatory gift surpasses its secrecy twenty-fivefold.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-256\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B228]<\/a><\/p><p>If you do as commanded in either manner, you have acted well, and “We efface some of your evil deeds”: We conceal some of your sins. It is transmitted that the giver of secret charity is shaded by God on the day when there is no shade except His shade. God is aware of what you do. Neither the one who conceals nor the one who discloses escapes His knowledge, nor the sincere person or the one with mixed motives. The words therefore encourage and warn. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: whatever you spend, little or much, secretly or openly, rightly or wrongly; or whatever vow you undertake, conditionally or unconditionally, in obedience or disobedience—God knows it and will recompense you. Whoever spends in obedience or vows a pious act is among those who do good. Whoever spends in disobedience or vows disobedience is among the wrongdoers, who have no helpers to save them from God’s punishment. If you disclose your charities sincerely, how excellent their disclosure is—especially for someone whose example is followed, for whom it is preferable. If you conceal them and give to the poor secretly, that is better, being nearer to sincerity. This concerns voluntary charity: its secrecy surpasses its publicity seventyfold. Obligatory giving requires a distinction. Someone fearing an admixture of ostentation should conceal it or act through a deputy; someone secure from that should make it public. It is transmitted that the publicity of an obligatory gift surpasses its secrecy twenty-fivefold. [B228] If you do as commanded in either manner, you have acted well, and “We efface some of your evil deeds”: We conceal some of your sins. It is transmitted that the giver of secret charity is shaded by God on the day when there is no shade except His shade. God is aware of what you do. Neither the one who conceals nor the one who discloses escapes His knowledge, nor the sincere person or the one with mixed motives. The words therefore encourage and warn. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Seeking wages, seeking unveiled communion","html":"<p>The servant’s dealings with his Master are either to seek rewards or to lift veils. The first is given his wages from behind the door; the second enters with the beloved. Someone who works for this world wrongs himself, and the wrongdoers have no helpers. A transmitted saying declares: “The seeker of the world is a captive; the seeker of the Hereafter a hired worker; the seeker of the Real a prince.”<\/p><p>People fall into three classes in their dealings with the Real. Concealment and secrecy befit disciples travelling in search of sincerity. Disclosure befits sincere scholars whose example is followed. A third class stops at neither disclosure nor concealment, but at what the moment brings forth: the complete gnostics.<\/p><p>Hence Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās said: “Whoever loves to be seen is a servant of being seen. Whoever loves concealment is a servant of concealment. For someone who is God’s servant, it is the same whether He makes him manifest or conceals him.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-257\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B229]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The servant’s dealings with his Master are either to seek rewards or to lift veils. The first is given his wages from behind the door; the second enters with the beloved. Someone who works for this world wrongs himself, and the wrongdoers have no helpers. A transmitted saying declares: “The seeker of the world is a captive; the seeker of the Hereafter a hired worker; the seeker of the Real a prince.” People fall into three classes in their dealings with the Real. Concealment and secrecy befit disciples travelling in search of sincerity. Disclosure befits sincere scholars whose example is followed. A third class stops at neither disclosure nor concealment, but at what the moment brings forth: the complete gnostics. Hence Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās said: “Whoever loves to be seen is a servant of being seen. Whoever loves concealment is a servant of concealment. For someone who is God’s servant, it is the same whether He makes him manifest or conceals him.” [B229]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>All guidance is in God’s hand; no other possesses any of it, as the Real, glorious is His majesty, explains next.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"All guidance is in God’s hand; no other possesses any of it, as the Real, glorious is His majesty, explains next."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4078,"arabic_characters":2912},{"id":"q2-272","q":"2:272","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:272 · Guidance belongs to God; the fruits of giving return to the giver","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 305–306","cairo":"I:305–306","source_page_first":305,"source_page_last":306,"ar":"لَيْسَ عَلَيْكَ هُداهُمْ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لنبيه عليه الصلاة والسلام: لَيْسَ عَلَيْكَ يا محمد هُداهُمْ أي: لا يجب عليك أن تخلق الهداية في قلوبهم، وليس من شأنك ذلك، إنما أنت نذير تدلُّ على الخير، كالنفقة وغيرها، وتنهى عن الشر كالمِّن والأذى، وإنفاق الخبيث، وغير ذلك من المساوئ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ بفضله وإحسانه، فالأمور كلها بيد الله خيرها وشرها، ولكن من جهة الأدب مَّآ أَصابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ وَما أَصابَكَ مِنْ سَيِّئَةٍ فَمِنْ نَفْسِكَ. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: ما قيل في الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- يقال في ورثته من أهل التذكير، فليس بيدهم الهداية والتوفيق، وإنما شأنهم الإرشاد وبيان الطريق، فليس من شأن الدعاة إلى الله الحرص على هداية الخلق. وإنما من\nشأنهم بيان الحقّ. إِنْ تَحْرِصْ عَلى هُداهُمْ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ لاَ يَهْدِي مَنْ يُضِلُّ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم رجع الحقّ تعالى إلى الترغيب في الصدقة والإخلاص فيها، فقال:\n... وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِأَنْفُسِكُمْ وَما تُنْفِقُونَ إِلاَّ ابْتِغاءَ وَجْهِ اللَّهِ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يُوَفَّ إِلَيْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لاَ تُظْلَمُونَ (272)\nقلت: هذه ثلاث جمل كلها تدل على الترغيب في إنفاق الطيب وإخلاص النية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ قليل أو كثير، فهو فَلِأَنْفُسِكُمْ لا ينتفع به غيركم، فإن كان طيباً فلأنفسكم، وإن كان خبيثاً فأجره لكم، وإن مننتم به أو آذيتم فقد ظلمتم أنفسكم، وإن أخلصتم فيه فلأنفسكم.\nوأيضاً إنكم تَدَّعُونَ أنكم ما تُنْفِقُونَ إِلَّا ابْتِغاءَ وَجْهِ اللَّهِ، فكيف تقصدون الخبيث، وتجعلونه لوجه الله؟ وكيف تَمُنُّونَ أو تؤذون بها وهى لوجه الله؟ هذا تكذيب للدعوى، وكل ما تنفقون من خيرٍ قليل أو كثير يُوَفَّ إِلَيْكُمْ جزاؤه يوم القيامة بسبعمائة إلى أضعاف كثيرة، ويخلفه لكم في الدنيا، وَأَنْتُمْ لا تُظْلَمُونَ شيئاً من أعمالكم إن أخلصتم أو أحسنتم. وستأتي إشارتها مع ما بعدها.\nثم بيّن المصرف، فقال:","quran_ar":"لَيْسَ عَلَيْكَ هُداهُمْ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِأَنْفُسِكُمْ وَما تُنْفِقُونَ إِلاَّ ابْتِغاءَ وَجْهِ اللَّهِ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يُوَفَّ إِلَيْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لاَ تُظْلَمُونَ (272)","quran_en":"272. Their guidance is not your responsibility; God guides whom He wills. Whatever good you spend is for yourselves, and you spend only seeking God’s Face. Whatever good you spend will be repaid to you in full, and you will not be wronged.","segments":[{"type":"theology","title":"The messenger indicates; God creates guidance","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to His Prophet: their guidance is not your responsibility, Muḥammad. You are not required to create guidance in their hearts, nor is that your function. You are only a warner, pointing towards good, such as expenditure and other acts, and forbidding evil, such as reminders, injury, giving inferior things and other faults.<\/p><p>God, however, guides whom He wills through His bounty and goodness. All affairs, good and evil, are in God’s hand. As a matter of proper manners, however: “Whatever good befalls you is from God, and whatever evil befalls you is from your self” [4:79]. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to His Prophet: their guidance is not your responsibility, Muḥammad. You are not required to create guidance in their hearts, nor is that your function. You are only a warner, pointing towards good, such as expenditure and other acts, and forbidding evil, such as reminders, injury, giving inferior things and other faults. God, however, guides whom He wills through His bounty and goodness. All affairs, good and evil, are in God’s hand. As a matter of proper manners, however: “Whatever good befalls you is from God, and whatever evil befalls you is from your self” [4:79]. And success is through God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The inheritors’ task is to make the path clear","html":"<p>What is said concerning the Messenger applies to his inheritors among those who give reminders. Guidance and enabling success are not in their hands. Their task is direction and clarification of the path. Those who call to God should not be anxiously intent upon bringing creation to guidance;<\/p><p>their task is to make the truth clear. “However eager you are for their guidance, God does not guide those He leaves astray” [16:37]. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><p>The Real Most High then returns to encouraging charity and sincerity within it.<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:272b\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:272b<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِأَنْفُسِكُمْ وَما تُنْفِقُونَ إِلَّا ابْتِغاءَ وَجْهِ اللَّهِ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يُوَفَّ إِلَيْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تُظْلَمُونَ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">Whatever good you spend is for yourselves, and you spend only seeking God’s Face. Whatever good you spend will be repaid to you in full, and you will not be wronged.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"What is said concerning the Messenger applies to his inheritors among those who give reminders. Guidance and enabling success are not in their hands. Their task is direction and clarification of the path. Those who call to God should not be anxiously intent upon bringing creation to guidance; their task is to make the truth clear. “However eager you are for their guidance, God does not guide those He leaves astray” [16:37]. And God Most High knows best. The Real Most High then returns to encouraging charity and sincerity within it. Qurʾānic continuation · 2:272b وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِأَنْفُسِكُمْ وَما تُنْفِقُونَ إِلَّا ابْتِغاءَ وَجْهِ اللَّهِ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يُوَفَّ إِلَيْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تُظْلَمُونَ Whatever good you spend is for yourselves, and you spend only seeking God’s Face. Whatever good you spend will be repaid to you in full, and you will not be wronged."},{"type":"language","title":"The claim of sincerity and its demands","html":"<p>I say: these three sentences all encourage giving what is good with a sincere intention.<\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: whatever good you spend, little or much, is for yourselves; its benefit accrues to you, not another. If it is good, it is for yourselves; if it is bad, its recompense is yours. If you remind the recipient or injure him, you wrong yourselves; if you act sincerely, it is for yourselves.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-258\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B230]<\/a><\/p><p>Moreover, you claim that you spend only seeking God’s Face. How, then, can you deliberately select the bad and make it an offering for His Face? How can you remind or injure through a gift supposedly given for His Face? That belies the claim.<\/p><p>Whatever good you spend, little or much, will be repaid in full at the Resurrection, seven hundredfold and many times beyond; He also replaces it for you in this world. You will be wronged in none of your deeds if you act sincerely or well. The spiritual allusion to this will follow together with the next verse.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: these three sentences all encourage giving what is good with a sincere intention. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: whatever good you spend, little or much, is for yourselves; its benefit accrues to you, not another. If it is good, it is for yourselves; if it is bad, its recompense is yours. If you remind the recipient or injure him, you wrong yourselves; if you act sincerely, it is for yourselves. [B230] Moreover, you claim that you spend only seeking God’s Face. How, then, can you deliberately select the bad and make it an offering for His Face? How can you remind or injure through a gift supposedly given for His Face? That belies the claim. Whatever good you spend, little or much, will be repaid in full at the Resurrection, seven hundredfold and many times beyond; He also replaces it for you in this world. You will be wronged in none of your deeds if you act sincerely or well. The spiritual allusion to this will follow together with the next verse."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then identifies the recipients.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then identifies the recipients."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2557,"arabic_characters":1773},{"id":"q2-273","q":"2:273","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:273 · The unobtrusive poor and support for those devoted to God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 306–308","cairo":"I:306–308","source_page_first":306,"source_page_last":308,"ar":"لِلْفُقَراءِ الَّذِينَ أُحْصِرُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ لا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ ضَرْباً فِي الْأَرْضِ يَحْسَبُهُمُ الْجاهِلُ أَغْنِياءَ مِنَ التَّعَفُّفِ تَعْرِفُهُمْ بِسِيماهُمْ لا يَسْئَلُونَ النَّاسَ إِلْحافاً وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (273)\nقلت: (للفقراء) : متعلق بمحذوف، أي: يعطي ذلك للفقراء، أو اجعلوا ما تنفقونه للفقراء، والإلحاف: هو الإلحاح في السؤال، وهو أن يلازم المسئول حتى يعطيه، وهو منصوب على المصدر أو الحال.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: تجعلون ما تنفقونه لِلْفُقَراءِ الَّذِينَ أُحْصِرُوا أي: حبسوا أنفسهم في سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وهو الجهاد، لا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ ضَرْباً فِي الْأَرْضِ أي: ذهاباً في الأرض للتجارة أو للأسباب، بل شغلهم الجهاد والتبتل للعبادة عن الأسباب، وهم أهل الصُّفَّة، كانوا نحواً من أربعمائة من فقراء المهاجرين، يسكنون صفة المسجد، يستغرقون أوقاتهم في العلم والذكر والعبادة، وكانوا يخرجون في كل سرية بعثها رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم.\nقال ابن عباس رضي الله عنه: «وقف النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يوماً على أصحاب الصفة، فرأى فقرهم وجهدهم وطيب قلوبهم، فقال: «أبشروا يا أصحاب الصفة، فمن بقي من أمتي على النعت الذي أنتم عليه، راضياً بما فيه فإنه، من رفقائي» .\nوقيل: المراد الفقراء مطلقاً، حصرهم الفقر عن الضرب في الأرض للتجارة، يَحْسَبُهُمُ الْجاهِلُ بهم أَغْنِياءَ مِنَ التَّعَفُّفِ، أي: من أجل تعففهم عن السؤال، تَعْرِفُهُمْ بِسِيماهُمْ من الضعف ورثاثة الحال.\nالخطاب للرسول، أو لكل أحدٍ لا يَسْئَلُونَ النَّاسَ إِلْحافاً، أي: لا يسألون، وإن سألوا عن ضرورة لم يلحوا، وقيل: نفى للأمرين معا، أي: ليس لهم سؤال، فيقع فيه إلحاف، كقول الشاعر:\nعلى لا حِبٍ لا يُهْتَدى بمنَارِه(1)\nوليس ثَمَّ لا حب ولا منار، وإنما المراد نفيهما، وفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «منْ سأَلَ، وله أربعونَ دِرهماً، فَقَدْ سأل إلحافاً» .\nوَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ فيجازي على القليل والكثير، وهذا ترغيب في الإنفاق، وخصوصاً على هؤلاء.\nالإشارة: ما أفلح مَن أفلح، وخسر مَنْ خسر، إلا من نفسه وفلسه، فمن جاد بهما، أو بأحدهما، فقد فاز وأفلح وظفر بما قصد، والجود بالنفس أعظم، وهو يستلزم الجود بالفلس، والجود بالفلس، إن دام، يوصل إلى الجود بالنفس، والمراد بالجود بالنفس: إسلامها للشيخ يفعل بها ما يشاء، وتكون الإشارة فيها كافية عن التصريح، ومن بخل بهما أو بأحدهما، فقد خسر وخاب في طريق الخصوص، ومصرف ذلك هو الشيخ، أو الفقراء المنقطعون إلى الله الذين حصروا أنفسهم في سبيل الله، وهو الجهاد الأكبر.\nقال في القوت: وكان بعض الفضلاء يؤثر بالعطاء فقراء الصوفية دون غيرهم، فقيل له في ذلك، فقال: لأن هؤلاء همهم الله عزّ وجلّ، فإذا ظهر منهم فاقة تَشَتَّتَ قلبُ أحدِهم، فلأنْ أرد هِمة واحدٍ إلى الله أحب إليَّ من أن أعطي ألفاً من غيرهم ممن همه الدنيا. فذكر هذا الكلام لأبي القاسم الجنيد، فقال: هذا كلام وليّ من أولياء الله. ثم قال: ما سمعت كلاماً أحسنَ من هذا. وبلغني أن هذا الرجل اقتر حاله في أمر الدنيا\nحتى همّ بترك الحانوت فبعث إليه الجنيد بمال كان صرف إليه، وقال له: اجعل هذا في بضاعتك، ولا تترك الحانوت فإنَّ التجارة لا تضرُّ مثلك. ويقال: إن هذا لم يكن يأخذ من الفقراء ثمن ما يبتاعون منه. هـ.\nوكان عبد الله بن المبارك يصرف مصروفه لأهل العلم، ويقول: إني لا أعرف بعد النبوة أفضل من العلماء، فإذا اشتغل قلب أحدهم بالحاجة والعيلة لم يتفرغ للعلم، ولا يقبل على تعليم الناس، فرأيت أن أكفيهم أمر الدنيا لأفرغهم للعلم، فهو أفضل. هـ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم رغّب فى النفقة مطلقا، فقال:","quran_ar":"لِلْفُقَراءِ الَّذِينَ أُحْصِرُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ لا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ ضَرْباً فِي الْأَرْضِ يَحْسَبُهُمُ الْجاهِلُ أَغْنِياءَ مِنَ التَّعَفُّفِ تَعْرِفُهُمْ بِسِيماهُمْ لا يَسْئَلُونَ النَّاسَ إِلْحافاً وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (273)","quran_en":"273. Give to the poor who are constrained in God’s path and cannot travel through the land. Someone unaware supposes them wealthy because of their restraint. You recognise them by their marks. They do not ask people insistently. Whatever good you spend, God knows it.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The recipients and persistent asking","html":"<p>I say: “For the poor” attaches to an omitted expression: this is given to the poor, or assign what you spend to the poor. <i>Ilḥāf<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإلحاف<\/bdi>) means insistence in asking: remaining with the person asked until he gives. It is accusative as an absolute accusative or a circumstance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “For the poor” attaches to an omitted expression: this is given to the poor, or assign what you spend to the poor. Ilḥāf ( الإلحاف ) means insistence in asking: remaining with the person asked until he gives. It is accusative as an absolute accusative or a circumstance."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Those who cannot pursue a livelihood","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: assign what you spend to the poor who have confined themselves to God’s path—fighting in His cause—and cannot travel through the land for trade or means of livelihood. Fighting and exclusive devotion to worship have occupied them away from earning. These are the People of the Bench (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الصفة<\/bdi>), about four hundred poor Emigrants who lived on the mosque’s covered platform. They devoted their time to knowledge, remembrance and worship, and went out with every expedition sent by the Messenger of God.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-259\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B231]<\/a><\/p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās related: “The Prophet stood one day before the People of the Bench and saw their poverty, hardship and purity of heart. He said: ‘Rejoice, People of the Bench. Whoever of my community remains in the condition you are in, content with it, is among my companions.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-260\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B231]<\/a><\/p><p>Another explanation takes the verse to concern the poor generally, whose poverty prevents travel for trade. Someone ignorant of their situation supposes them wealthy because of their restraint from asking. You recognise them by their marks: weakness and a shabby appearance. The address is to the Messenger or to everyone.<\/p><p>“They do not ask people insistently”: they do not ask, or, if necessity makes them ask, they do not persist. Another explanation negates both asking and insistence: they make no request in which insistence could occur. It resembles the poet’s words:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Upon a clear path by whose beacons one is not guided—<\/blockquote><p>where, according to this explanation, there is neither clear path nor beacon: both are negated. A report from the Prophet says: “Whoever asks while possessing forty dirhams has asked insistently.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-261\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B231]<\/a><\/p><p>Whatever good you spend, God knows it, recompensing little and much. This encourages expenditure, especially upon such people.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: assign what you spend to the poor who have confined themselves to God’s path—fighting in His cause—and cannot travel through the land for trade or means of livelihood. Fighting and exclusive devotion to worship have occupied them away from earning. These are the People of the Bench ( أهل الصفة ), about four hundred poor Emigrants who lived on the mosque’s covered platform. They devoted their time to knowledge, remembrance and worship, and went out with every expedition sent by the Messenger of God. [B231] Ibn ʿAbbās related: “The Prophet stood one day before the People of the Bench and saw their poverty, hardship and purity of heart. He said: ‘Rejoice, People of the Bench. Whoever of my community remains in the condition you are in, content with it, is among my companions.’” [B231] Another explanation takes the verse to concern the poor generally, whose poverty prevents travel for trade. Someone ignorant of their situation supposes them wealthy because of their restraint from asking. You recognise them by their marks: weakness and a shabby appearance. The address is to the Messenger or to everyone. “They do not ask people insistently”: they do not ask, or, if necessity makes them ask, they do not persist. Another explanation negates both asking and insistence: they make no request in which insistence could occur. It resembles the poet’s words: Upon a clear path by whose beacons one is not guided— where, according to this explanation, there is neither clear path nor beacon: both are negated. A report from the Prophet says: “Whoever asks while possessing forty dirhams has asked insistently.” [B231] Whatever good you spend, God knows it, recompensing little and much. This encourages expenditure, especially upon such people."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Giving oneself and one’s means","html":"<p>Those who succeed and those who lose do so through their self and their money. Whoever gives both, or either, gains success and obtains his aim. Giving the self is greater and entails giving money. Giving money, if sustained, leads to giving the self.<\/p><p>Giving the self means surrendering it to the shaykh, who acts upon it as he wills; a mere indication from him suffices without explicit instruction. Whoever withholds both or either loses and fails on the path of the elect. The recipients are the shaykh or the poor devoted entirely to God, who have confined themselves to His path: the greater struggle.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Those who succeed and those who lose do so through their self and their money. Whoever gives both, or either, gains success and obtains his aim. Giving the self is greater and entails giving money. Giving money, if sustained, leads to giving the self. Giving the self means surrendering it to the shaykh, who acts upon it as he wills; a mere indication from him suffices without explicit instruction. Whoever withholds both or either loses and fails on the path of the elect. The recipients are the shaykh or the poor devoted entirely to God, who have confined themselves to His path: the greater struggle."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Supporting contemplatives and scholars","html":"<p>The <i>Qūt<\/i> relates that a virtuous man preferred poor Sufis over others in giving. Asked why, he replied: “Their concern is God, mighty and majestic. When need appears, one of their hearts becomes scattered. Restoring one person’s aspiration to God is dearer to me than giving to a thousand others whose concern is the world.” These words were reported to Abū al-Qāsim al-Junayd, who said: “These are the words of one of God’s saints. I have heard no finer words.”<\/p><p>It reached the author that this man’s worldly circumstances became so straitened<\/p><p>that he considered closing his shop. Al-Junayd sent him money that had come to him, saying: “Put this into your stock and do not abandon the shop. Trade does not harm someone like you.” It is said that he would not take payment from the poor for what they bought from him. Here the quotation ends.<\/p><p>ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak directed his expenditure towards scholars. He said: “After prophecy, I know nothing better than scholars. If one of their hearts becomes occupied with need and dependants, he cannot devote himself to knowledge or turn towards teaching people. I therefore thought it best to relieve them of worldly concerns and free them for knowledge.” And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Qūt relates that a virtuous man preferred poor Sufis over others in giving. Asked why, he replied: “Their concern is God, mighty and majestic. When need appears, one of their hearts becomes scattered. Restoring one person’s aspiration to God is dearer to me than giving to a thousand others whose concern is the world.” These words were reported to Abū al-Qāsim al-Junayd, who said: “These are the words of one of God’s saints. I have heard no finer words.” It reached the author that this man’s worldly circumstances became so straitened that he considered closing his shop. Al-Junayd sent him money that had come to him, saying: “Put this into your stock and do not abandon the shop. Trade does not harm someone like you.” It is said that he would not take payment from the poor for what they bought from him. Here the quotation ends. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak directed his expenditure towards scholars. He said: “After prophecy, I know nothing better than scholars. If one of their hearts becomes occupied with need and dependants, he cannot devote himself to knowledge or turn towards teaching people. I therefore thought it best to relieve them of worldly concerns and free them for knowledge.” And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then encourages expenditure generally.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then encourages expenditure generally."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C307.1","page":307,"ar":"هذا صدر بيت عجزه: (إذا سافه العود النباطي جرجرا) وهو من قصيدة لامرئ القيس. واللاحب: الطريق الواسع.","en":"This is the first half of a verse whose second half is: “When the old Nabataean camel scents it, it rumbles.” It is from a poem by Imruʾ al-Qays. Lāḥib means a broad road.","anchor":"على لا حِبٍ لا يُهْتَدى بمنَارِه"}],"english_characters":3948,"arabic_characters":3097},{"id":"q2-274","q":"2:274","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:274 · Giving by night and day","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 308–308","cairo":"I:308","source_page_first":308,"source_page_last":308,"ar":"الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ بِاللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهارِ سِرًّا وَعَلانِيَةً فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (274)\nقلت: الموصول مبتدأ، و (فلهم أجرهم) : خبر، والفاء للسببية، ولأن في الموصول معنى الشرط، وقيل: الخبر محذوف، أي: ومنهم الذين ينفقون الخ، و (فلهم) : استئناف بياني.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ بِاللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهارِ سِرًّا وَعَلانِيَةً، ويعمرون أوقاتهم بفعل الخيرات، فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ إذا قدموا عليه، وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ من لحوق مكروه، وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ على فوات محبوب، بل وجدوا الله فأغناهم عن كل شيء.\nقيل: نزلت في أبي بكر رضي الله عنه تصدَّق بأربعين ألف دينار، عشرة بالليل، وعشرة بالنهار، وعشرة بالسر، وعشرة بالعلانية، أو في علي- كرم الله وجهه- لم يملك إلا أربعة دراهم، فتصدق بدرهم ليلاً، ودرهم نهاراً، ودرهم سرّاً، ودرهم علانية. وهي عامة لمن فعل فعلهما.\nالإشارة: أجر بذل الأموال هو إعطاء الثواب من وراء الباب، والأمن من العذاب وسوء المآب، وأجر بذل النفوس هو دخول حضرة القدوس، والأنس بالأحباب داخل الحجاب، فمن بذل نفسه لله على الدوام، أمنه من الحجبة في دار السلام، فلا خوف يلحقهم في الدارين، ولا يعتريهم حزن في الكونين. وبالله التوفيق.\nولما رغَّب في الصدقة، وكانت في الغالب لا يتوصل إليها إلا بتعاطي أسباب المال، وهو البيع والشراء حذر من الربا لئلا يتساهل الناس في المعاملة به، حرصاً على الصدقة، فقال:","quran_ar":"الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ بِاللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهارِ سِرًّا وَعَلانِيَةً فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (274)","quran_en":"274. Those who spend their wealth by night and day, secretly and openly, have their reward with their Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The conditional force of the relative expression","html":"<p>I say: the relative expression is the subject and “they have their reward” the predicate. The <i>fāʾ<\/i> expresses causation and appears because the relative expression contains a conditional meaning. Alternatively, the predicate is omitted: “Among them are those who spend …”; “they have” then begins an explanatory new sentence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استئناف بياني<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the relative expression is the subject and “they have their reward” the predicate. The fāʾ expresses causation and appears because the relative expression contains a conditional meaning. Alternatively, the predicate is omitted: “Among them are those who spend …”; “they have” then begins an explanatory new sentence ( استئناف بياني )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A life filled with good deeds","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who spend their wealth by night and day, secretly and openly, filling their times with good deeds, have their reward with their Lord when they come to Him. They have no fear of something harmful overtaking them and do not grieve over a beloved thing lost. Rather, they have found God, who makes them independent of everything.<\/p><p>The verse is said to concern Abū Bakr, who gave forty thousand dinars in charity: ten thousand by night, ten thousand by day, ten thousand secretly and ten thousand openly. Another account identifies ʿAlī, who possessed only four dirhams and gave one by night, one by day, one secretly and one openly. The verse applies generally to anyone who acts as they did.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-262\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B232]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who spend their wealth by night and day, secretly and openly, filling their times with good deeds, have their reward with their Lord when they come to Him. They have no fear of something harmful overtaking them and do not grieve over a beloved thing lost. Rather, they have found God, who makes them independent of everything. The verse is said to concern Abū Bakr, who gave forty thousand dinars in charity: ten thousand by night, ten thousand by day, ten thousand secretly and ten thousand openly. Another account identifies ʿAlī, who possessed only four dirhams and gave one by night, one by day, one secretly and one openly. The verse applies generally to anyone who acts as they did. [B232]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Beyond reward at the door","html":"<p>The reward of giving wealth is recompense from behind the door, and security from punishment and an evil return. The reward of giving the self is entrance into the presence of the All-Holy and intimacy with the beloved within the veil. Whoever continually gives his self to God is made secure from veiling in the Abode of Peace. No fear reaches them in either abode, and no grief befalls them in either world. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The reward of giving wealth is recompense from behind the door, and security from punishment and an evil return. The reward of giving the self is entrance into the presence of the All-Holy and intimacy with the beloved within the veil. Whoever continually gives his self to God is made secure from veiling in the Abode of Peace. No fear reaches them in either abode, and no grief befalls them in either world. And success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having encouraged charity, which is generally attained through the means of earning wealth—buying and selling—He warns against usury, lest eagerness to give charity make people lax about dealing in it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having encouraged charity, which is generally attained through the means of earning wealth—buying and selling—He warns against usury, lest eagerness to give charity make people lax about dealing in it."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1721,"arabic_characters":1319},{"id":"q2-275-279","q":"2:275–279","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:275–279 · Usury, the lawful exchange and the purity of one’s sustenance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 309–311","cairo":"I:309–311","source_page_first":309,"source_page_last":311,"ar":"الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ الرِّبا لا يَقُومُونَ إِلاَّ كَما يَقُومُ الَّذِي يَتَخَبَّطُهُ الشَّيْطانُ مِنَ الْمَسِّ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ قالُوا إِنَّمَا الْبَيْعُ مِثْلُ الرِّبا وَأَحَلَّ اللَّهُ الْبَيْعَ وَحَرَّمَ الرِّبا فَمَنْ جاءَهُ مَوْعِظَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِ فَانْتَهى فَلَهُ ما سَلَفَ وَأَمْرُهُ إِلَى اللَّهِ وَمَنْ عادَ فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (275) يَمْحَقُ اللَّهُ الرِّبا وَيُرْبِي الصَّدَقاتِ وَاللَّهُ لاَ يُحِبُّ كُلَّ كَفَّارٍ أَثِيمٍ (276) إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ وَأَقامُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتَوُا الزَّكاةَ لَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (277) \nقلت: (الربا) في الأصل: هو الزيادة، رَبا المال يربو: زاد. وكتبت بالواو مراعاة للأصل، وهو المصدر، قال الفراء: إنما كتبوه بالواو لأن أهل الحجاز تعلموا الكتابة من أهل الحيرة، ولغتهم الربو، فعلموهم صورة الحروف، وكذلك قرأها أبو السمال العدوي، وقرأ الأخَوَان بالإمالة لمكان الكسرة، والباقون بالتفخيم.\nوالربا في اصطلاح الشرع على قسمين: ربا الفضل وربا النِّسَاءِ، فأما ربا الفضل فهو التفاضل بين الطعامين أو النقدين في المبادلة من الجنس الواحد، فإن اختلفت الأجناس فلا حرج، وأما ربا النساء فهو بيع الطعامين أو النقدين بعضهما ببعض بالتأخير، وهذا حرام ولو اختلفت الأجناس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ الرِّبا أي: يأخذونه، وإنما خص الأكل لأنه أعظم منافع المال، لا يَقُومُونَ من قبورهم يوم البعث إِلَّا كَما يَقُومُ المجنون الَّذِي يَتَخَبَّطُهُ الشَّيْطانُ مِنَ أجل الْمَسِّ الذي يمسه يقوم ويسقط، رُوِيَ أن بطونهم تكون أمامهم كالبيت الضخم، يقوم أحدهم فتميل به بطنه فيصرع، وعن أبى هريرة رضي الله عنه أنه قال: قال رسول الله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «لما أُسْرِيَ بِي إلى السماء رأيتُ رِجالاً بُطُونُهُمْ كَالبُيوتِ، فيهَا حَيَّاتٌ تُرى مِنْ خِارِجِ بُطُونِهمْ، فقلت: مَنْ هؤلاءِ يا جِبْرِيل؟ فقال: أكَلَةُ الرِّبا» .\nذلِكَ العذاب بسبب أنهم استحلُّوا الربا، وقالُوا إِنَّمَا الْبَيْعُ مِثْلُ الرِّبا فنظموا الربا والبيع في سلك واحد، وفيه عكس التشبيه. والأصل: إنما الربا مثل البيع، قصدوا المبالغة، كأنهم جعلوا الربا أصلاً وقاسوا عليه البيع.\nوذلك أن أهل الجاهلية كان أحدهم إذا حلّ ماله على غريمه يقول الغريم: زدني في الأجل أزدك في المال، فيفعلان، ويقولان: سواء علينا الزيادة في أول البيع بالربح أو عند محل الدين، هو مراضاة. فكذبهم الحقّ تعالى بقوله: وَأَحَلَّ اللَّهُ الْبَيْعَ وَحَرَّمَ الرِّبا لأن القياس مع وجود النص فاسد، والفرق ظاهر فإن من باع درهماً\nبدرهمين ضيع درهماً من غير فائدة، بخلاف من اشترى سلعة بدرهم، وباعها بدرهمين، فلعل مساس الحاجة، والرغبة فيها، توقع رواجها فيجبر الغبن.\nفَمَنْ جاءَهُ مَوْعِظَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِ كالنهي عن الربا، فَانْتَهى وترك الربا فَلَهُ ما سَلَفَ قبل التحريم ولا يَرُدُّهُ، وَأَمْرُهُ إِلَى اللَّهِ لا إلى أحد منكم، فلا يتعرض له، وَمَنْ عادَ إلى تحليل الربا بعد بلوغه النهي فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ لأنهم كفروا وسفهوا أمر الله. يَمْحَقُ اللَّهُ الرِّبا أي: يذهب بركته، ويُهلك المال الذي يدخل فيه وَيُرْبِي الصَّدَقاتِ أي: يضاعف ثوابها ويُبارك في المال الذي أخرجت منه، فقد رُوِيَ عنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- أنه قال: «ما نَقَصَ مالٌ مِنْ صَدقة» ، «وأنه يُربي الصدقةَ حتى تكونَ مثلَ الجبل» . قال يحيى بن معاذ: (ما أعرفُ حبةً تزن جبال الدنيا إلا الحبَّةَ من الصدقة) .\nوَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ كُلَّ كَفَّارٍ أي: مُصِرٍّ على تحليل المحرمات، أَثِيمٍ أي: منهمك في ارتكاب المنهيات، أي: لا يرتضي حاله، ولا يحبه كما يحب التوابين.\nثم ذكر مقابله فقال: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بالله، وصدَّقُوا بما جاء من عنده، وَعَمِلُوا الأعمال الصَّالِحاتِ وَأَقامُوا الصَّلاةَ أي: أتقنوها وَآتَوُا الزَّكاةَ أي: أدوها على التمام، فلهم أجرهم عند ربهم إذا قدموا عليه، وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ من آت، وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ على ما فات، إذ لم يفتهم شيء حيث وجدوا الله.\nثم أكد فى أمر الربا، فقال:\nيا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَذَرُوا ما بَقِيَ مِنَ الرِّبا أي: اتركوا بقايا ما شرطتم على الناس من الربا، فلا تقبضوها منهم، إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (278) . فإن دليل الإيمان: امتثال ما أمرتم به، رُوِيَ أنه كان لثقيف مال على بعض قريش، فطالبوهم عند الحَلِّ بالمال والربا، فنزلت الآية.\nفَإِنْ لَمْ تَفْعَلُوا وتتركوا ما نهيتم عنه، فَأْذَنُوا أي: فاعلموا بِحَرْبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ ومن قرأ:\nفآذنوا بالمد، فمعناه: أعلموا بها غيركم، رُوِيَ أنها لمّا نزلت، قالت ثقيف: لا يَدَانِ لنا بحرب الله ورسوله(1).\nوَإِنْ تُبْتُمْ من تعاطي الربا واعتقاد حله فَلَكُمْ رُؤُسُ أَمْوالِكُمْ لا تَظْلِمُونَ الغريم بأخذ الزيادة، وَلا تُظْلَمُونَ (279) بنقص رأس مالكم. مفهومه إن لم يتب فليس له شيء، لأنه مرتد. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: مدار صفاء المعاملة على تصفية اللقمة، فمن صفَّا طعمته صفت معاملته، ومن صفت معاملته أفضى الصفاء إلى قلبه، ومن خلط في لقمته تكدرت معاملته، ومن تكدرت معاملته تكدر قلبه، ولذلك قال بعضهم: (مَنْ أكَلَ الحلال أطاع الله، أحبَّ أم كَرِهَ، ومن أكل الحرام: عصى الله، أحبَّ أم كَرِه) وكذلك الواردات الإلهية، لا ترد إلا على من صفا مطعمه ومشربه، ولذلك قال بعضهم: (من لا يعرف ما يدخل بطنه لا يفرق بين الخواطر الربانية والشيطانية) .\nوقال سيدى على الخواص رضي الله عنه: (اعلم أن المدد الذي لم يزل فياضاً على قلب كل إنسان يتلون بحسب القلب، والقلب يتلون بحسبه هو بحسب صلاح الطعمة وفسادها) . هـ. فالذين يأكلون الحرام كالربا وشبهه، لا يقومون إلى معاملتهم للحق إلا كما يقوم المجنون الذي يلعب به الشيطان، ولا يدري ما يقول ولا ما يقال له، فقد حُرم لذيذ المناجاة وحلاوة خلوص المعاملات، فإن احتج لنفسه واستعمل القياس لم يُرْجَ فلاحُه في طريق الخواص، فمن جاءه موعظة من ربه فانتهى، وطلب العفاف فقد عفا الله عما سلف. ومن عاد إلى ما خرج عنه من متابعة هواه، فنار القطيعة مثواه ومأواه.\nومن شأن الحق جلّ جلاله مع عباده: أن من طلب الزيادة في حس ظاهره محق الله نور باطنه، ومن حسم مادة زيادة الحس في ظاهره قوي الله مدد الأنوار في باطنه، (يَمْحَقُ الله الربا ويربى الصدقات) ، أي: يُقَوِّي مدد ثواب الصدقات. (وَاللَّهُ لاَ يُحِبُّ كل كفّار أثيم) ، وإنما يحب كل مطيع منيب، وهو من آمن إيمان أهل التحقيق، وسلك مسلك أهل التوفيق. فلا جرم أنه ينخرط في سلك أهل العناية، ويسلك به مسلك أهل الولاية، (الذين لا خوف عليهم ولا هم يحزنون) .\n(يا أيها الذين آمنوا اتقوا الله) حق تقاته، واتركوا ما بقي في باطنكم من بقايا الحس وأسبابه، إن كنتم طالبين إيمان أهل الشهود، والوصول إلى الملك المعبود. فإن لم تفعلوا ذلك فاعلموا أنكم في مقام البعد من حيث لا تظنون، معاندون وأنتم لا تشعرون. وإن رجعتم إلى ربكم فلكم رؤوس أموالكم، وهو نور التوحيد، لا تنقصون منه ولا تزيدون عليه، إلا إن أفردتم الوجهة إليه، وطلبتم الوصول منه إليه، فإن الله لا يُخيٍّب من أمَّل جُوده، ولا يردُّ من وقَف ببابه، بمنِّه وكرمه.\nثم ذكر حال المعسر، فقال:","quran_ar":"الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ الرِّبا لا يَقُومُونَ إِلاَّ كَما يَقُومُ الَّذِي يَتَخَبَّطُهُ الشَّيْطانُ مِنَ الْمَسِّ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ قالُوا إِنَّمَا الْبَيْعُ مِثْلُ الرِّبا وَأَحَلَّ اللَّهُ الْبَيْعَ وَحَرَّمَ الرِّبا فَمَنْ جاءَهُ مَوْعِظَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِ فَانْتَهى فَلَهُ ما سَلَفَ وَأَمْرُهُ إِلَى اللَّهِ وَمَنْ عادَ فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (275) يَمْحَقُ اللَّهُ الرِّبا وَيُرْبِي الصَّدَقاتِ وَاللَّهُ لاَ يُحِبُّ كُلَّ كَفَّارٍ أَثِيمٍ (276) إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ وَأَقامُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتَوُا الزَّكاةَ لَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (277) يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَذَرُوا ما بَقِيَ مِنَ الرِّبا إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (278) فَإِنْ لَمْ تَفْعَلُوا فَأْذَنُوا بِحَرْبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَإِنْ تُبْتُمْ فَلَكُمْ رُؤُسُ أَمْوالِكُمْ لا تَظْلِمُونَ وَلا تُظْلَمُونَ (279)","quran_en":"275. Those who consume usury will rise only as one whom Satan has confounded through his touch. That is because they say, “Trade is only like usury,” although God has permitted trade and forbidden usury. Whoever receives an admonition from his Lord and desists may retain what is past; his affair belongs to God. Whoever returns—such are the people of the Fire, abiding therein. 276. God diminishes usury and causes charities to grow. God does not love any persistently ungrateful sinner. 277. Those who believe, do good deeds, establish prayer and give the alms have their reward with their Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve. 278. You who believe, be mindful of God and abandon what remains of usury, if you are believers. 279. If you do not, then be warned of war from God and His Messenger. If you repent, your principal is yours: you shall neither wrong nor be wronged.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The root of ribā and its two kinds","html":"<p>I say: <i>ribā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الربا<\/bdi>) originally means increase: property <i>rabā yarbū<\/i> when it increases. It is written with <i>wāw<\/i> in consideration of its original form as a verbal noun. Al-Farrāʾ said: they wrote it with <i>wāw<\/i> because the people of the Ḥijāz learned writing from the people of al-Ḥīra, whose usage was <i>ribū<\/i>. They taught them the letter-forms. Abū al-Sammāl al-ʿAdawī recited it thus. The two brothers recited it with vowel inclination (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإمالة<\/bdi>), because of the <i>kasra<\/i>; the others without inclination (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التفخيم<\/bdi>).<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-263\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B233]<\/a><\/p><p>In the technical usage of the Sacred Law, usury has two kinds: surplus usury (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ربا الفضل<\/bdi>) and deferred usury (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ربا النَّساء<\/bdi>). The first is an unequal exchange of foods or currencies within one genus; when the genera differ, there is no objection to inequality. The second is the exchange of foods or currencies with delayed delivery; this is forbidden even when the genera differ.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-264\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B233]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ribā ( الربا ) originally means increase: property rabā yarbū when it increases. It is written with wāw in consideration of its original form as a verbal noun. Al-Farrāʾ said: they wrote it with wāw because the people of the Ḥijāz learned writing from the people of al-Ḥīra, whose usage was ribū . They taught them the letter-forms. Abū al-Sammāl al-ʿAdawī recited it thus. The two brothers recited it with vowel inclination ( الإمالة ), because of the kasra ; the others without inclination ( التفخيم ). [B233] In the technical usage of the Sacred Law, usury has two kinds: surplus usury ( ربا الفضل ) and deferred usury ( ربا النَّساء ). The first is an unequal exchange of foods or currencies within one genus; when the genera differ, there is no objection to inequality. The second is the exchange of foods or currencies with delayed delivery; this is forbidden even when the genera differ. [B233]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The inverted comparison","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who consume usury—that is, take it; consumption is specified because it is the greatest use of wealth—will rise from their graves at the Resurrection only as one bereft of reason whom Satan throws into confusion through his touch, rising and falling. It is related that their bellies will be before them like enormous houses, so that each time they rise their bellies incline and throw them down. Abū Hurayra related that the Messenger of God said: “When I was taken by night to heaven, I saw men with bellies like houses, containing snakes visible from outside. I asked, ‘Who are these, Gabriel?’ He replied, ‘Those who consume usury.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-265\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B233]<\/a><\/p><p>This punishment is because they declared usury lawful and said: “Trade is only like usury,” placing both in one category. The comparison is inverted (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عكس التشبيه<\/bdi>). The expected form would be “Usury is like trade”; for emphasis, they make usury the original case and compare trade with it.<\/p><p>In the age of ignorance, when a debt fell due, the debtor would say: “Extend my term, and I shall increase the sum.” They would do so, saying: “An increase as profit at the beginning of the sale and an increase when the debt falls due are the same; both rest upon mutual consent.” The Real Most High declares this false: “God has permitted trade and forbidden usury.” Analogy in the face of an explicit text is invalid, and the distinction is manifest. Someone who sells a dirham<\/p><p>for two dirhams makes the other lose a dirham without benefit. Someone who buys merchandise for a dirham and sells it for two is different: pressing need, desire for the goods and the prospect of their marketability may compensate for the price disadvantage.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who consume usury—that is, take it; consumption is specified because it is the greatest use of wealth—will rise from their graves at the Resurrection only as one bereft of reason whom Satan throws into confusion through his touch, rising and falling. It is related that their bellies will be before them like enormous houses, so that each time they rise their bellies incline and throw them down. Abū Hurayra related that the Messenger of God said: “When I was taken by night to heaven, I saw men with bellies like houses, containing snakes visible from outside. I asked, ‘Who are these, Gabriel?’ He replied, ‘Those who consume usury.’” [B233] This punishment is because they declared usury lawful and said: “Trade is only like usury,” placing both in one category. The comparison is inverted ( عكس التشبيه ). The expected form would be “Usury is like trade”; for emphasis, they make usury the original case and compare trade with it. In the age of ignorance, when a debt fell due, the debtor would say: “Extend my term, and I shall increase the sum.” They would do so, saying: “An increase as profit at the beginning of the sale and an increase when the debt falls due are the same; both rest upon mutual consent.” The Real Most High declares this false: “God has permitted trade and forbidden usury.” Analogy in the face of an explicit text is invalid, and the distinction is manifest. Someone who sells a dirham for two dirhams makes the other lose a dirham without benefit. Someone who buys merchandise for a dirham and sells it for two is different: pressing need, desire for the goods and the prospect of their marketability may compensate for the price disadvantage."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Desisting, persistence and the growth of charity","html":"<p>Whoever receives an admonition from his Lord, such as the prohibition of usury, and desists, retains what preceded the prohibition and need not return it. His affair belongs to God, not any of you; he is not to be interfered with. Whoever returns to declaring usury lawful after the prohibition has reached him is among the people of the Fire, abiding therein, because he has disbelieved and treated God’s command as foolish.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-266\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B234]<\/a><\/p><p>God diminishes usury: removes its blessing and destroys the wealth into which it enters. He causes charities to grow: multiplies their reward and blesses the wealth from which they were taken. It is reported that the Prophet said: “Wealth is not diminished by charity,” and that God nurtures charity until it becomes like a mountain. Yaḥyā ibn Muʿādh said: “I know no grain weighing as much as the mountains of the world except a grain given in charity.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-267\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B234]<\/a><\/p><p>God does not love any persistent rejecter—one who persists in declaring forbidden things lawful—or sinner, immersed in prohibited acts. He does not approve his condition or love him as He loves those who repent.<\/p><p>He then describes their opposite: those who believe in God and affirm what comes from Him, do righteous deeds, perfect prayer and pay the alms completely have their reward with their Lord when they come to Him. They fear nothing to come and grieve over nothing past; they have missed nothing, having found God.<\/p><p>He then reinforces the prohibition of usury:<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:278–279\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:278–279<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَذَرُوا ما بَقِيَ مِنَ الرِّبا إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ فَإِنْ لَمْ تَفْعَلُوا فَأْذَنُوا بِحَرْبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَإِنْ تُبْتُمْ فَلَكُمْ رُؤُسُ أَمْوالِكُمْ لا تَظْلِمُونَ وَلا تُظْلَمُونَ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">You who believe, be mindful of God and abandon what remains of usury, if you are believers. If you do not, then be warned of war from God and His Messenger. If you repent, your principal is yours: you shall neither wrong nor be wronged.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Whoever receives an admonition from his Lord, such as the prohibition of usury, and desists, retains what preceded the prohibition and need not return it. His affair belongs to God, not any of you; he is not to be interfered with. Whoever returns to declaring usury lawful after the prohibition has reached him is among the people of the Fire, abiding therein, because he has disbelieved and treated God’s command as foolish. [B234] God diminishes usury: removes its blessing and destroys the wealth into which it enters. He causes charities to grow: multiplies their reward and blesses the wealth from which they were taken. It is reported that the Prophet said: “Wealth is not diminished by charity,” and that God nurtures charity until it becomes like a mountain. Yaḥyā ibn Muʿādh said: “I know no grain weighing as much as the mountains of the world except a grain given in charity.” [B234] God does not love any persistent rejecter—one who persists in declaring forbidden things lawful—or sinner, immersed in prohibited acts. He does not approve his condition or love him as He loves those who repent. He then describes their opposite: those who believe in God and affirm what comes from Him, do righteous deeds, perfect prayer and pay the alms completely have their reward with their Lord when they come to Him. They fear nothing to come and grieve over nothing past; they have missed nothing, having found God. He then reinforces the prohibition of usury: Qurʾānic continuation · 2:278–279 يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَذَرُوا ما بَقِيَ مِنَ الرِّبا إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ فَإِنْ لَمْ تَفْعَلُوا فَأْذَنُوا بِحَرْبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَإِنْ تُبْتُمْ فَلَكُمْ رُؤُسُ أَمْوالِكُمْ لا تَظْلِمُونَ وَلا تُظْلَمُونَ You who believe, be mindful of God and abandon what remains of usury, if you are believers. If you do not, then be warned of war from God and His Messenger. If you repent, your principal is yours: you shall neither wrong nor be wronged."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Abandon the outstanding increment","html":"<p>You who believe, be mindful of God and leave the outstanding usury you stipulated against people; do not collect it, if you are believers. The evidence of faith is compliance with what you are commanded. It is related that Thaqīf had money owed to them by some of Quraysh. When it fell due, they demanded the principal and usury, and the verse was revealed.<\/p><p>If you do not abandon what you have been forbidden, know of war from God and His Messenger. The reading <i>fa-ādhinū<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فآذنوا<\/bdi>), with lengthening, means inform others of it. When the verse was revealed, Thaqīf are reported to have said: “We have no power to wage war against God and His Messenger.”<\/p><p>If you repent of taking usury and believing it lawful, your principal is yours. You do not wrong the debtor by taking an increment, and you are not wronged by a reduction in your principal. The implication is that one who does not repent has no entitlement, because he is an apostate. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-268\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B234]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"You who believe, be mindful of God and leave the outstanding usury you stipulated against people; do not collect it, if you are believers. The evidence of faith is compliance with what you are commanded. It is related that Thaqīf had money owed to them by some of Quraysh. When it fell due, they demanded the principal and usury, and the verse was revealed. If you do not abandon what you have been forbidden, know of war from God and His Messenger. The reading fa-ādhinū ( فآذنوا ), with lengthening, means inform others of it. When the verse was revealed, Thaqīf are reported to have said: “We have no power to wage war against God and His Messenger.” If you repent of taking usury and believing it lawful, your principal is yours. You do not wrong the debtor by taking an increment, and you are not wronged by a reduction in your principal. The implication is that one who does not repent has no entitlement, because he is an apostate. And God Most High knows best. [B234]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Purity of sustenance and purity of inward reception","html":"<p>Pure dealings with God turn upon purifying one’s morsel. Whoever makes his food pure has pure dealings; their purity reaches his heart. Whoever mixes impurity into his morsel clouds his dealings, and that clouds his heart. Hence the saying: “Whoever eats lawful food obeys God, willingly or unwillingly; whoever eats unlawful food disobeys God, willingly or unwillingly.”<\/p><p>Likewise, divine incoming inspirations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواردات الإلهية<\/bdi>) arrive only upon someone whose food and drink are pure. One of them said: “Someone who does not know what enters his belly cannot distinguish lordly impulses from satanic ones.” Sīdī ʿAlī al-Khawwāṣ said: “Know that spiritual support continually overflows upon every human heart and takes its colour according to the heart. The heart takes its colour accordingly, in keeping with the goodness or corruption of its sustenance.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-269\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B235]<\/a><\/p><p>Those who eat unlawful things, such as usury and its like, rise to their dealings with the Real only as a person bereft of reason with whom Satan plays, not knowing what he says or what is said to him. He is deprived of the delight of intimate converse and the sweetness of pure dealings. If he argues in his own defence and resorts to analogy, no success is to be hoped for him on the path of the elect. Whoever receives an admonition from his Lord, desists and seeks purity has his past pardoned. Whoever returns to following the desire he had abandoned has the fire of severance as his dwelling and refuge.<\/p><p>The Real’s way with His servants is that when someone seeks an increase in the sensory dimension of his outward being, God diminishes the light of his inward being. When someone cuts off the supply feeding this outward sensory increase, God strengthens the inflow of lights within him. “God diminishes usury and causes charities to grow”: He strengthens the support conveyed by charity’s reward. God does not love any persistent rejecter and sinner, but every obedient one who returns to Him: someone who believes with the faith of realized people and follows those granted success. Such a person is necessarily threaded into the company of divine care and taken upon the path of protecting friendship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>): those upon whom there is no fear and who do not grieve.<\/p><p>You who believe, be mindful of God as He should be, and leave whatever traces of sensory attachment and its causes remain within you, if you seek the faith of direct witnessing and arrival at the worshipped King. If you do not, know that you remain distant without realising it, opposing without being aware. If you return to your Lord, your principal is yours: the light of unity. You neither diminish it nor add to it—unless you direct yourselves wholly towards Him and seek arrival through Him at Him. God does not disappoint one who hopes for His generosity or turn away one who stands at His door, through His grace and bounty.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Pure dealings with God turn upon purifying one’s morsel. Whoever makes his food pure has pure dealings; their purity reaches his heart. Whoever mixes impurity into his morsel clouds his dealings, and that clouds his heart. Hence the saying: “Whoever eats lawful food obeys God, willingly or unwillingly; whoever eats unlawful food disobeys God, willingly or unwillingly.” Likewise, divine incoming inspirations ( الواردات الإلهية ) arrive only upon someone whose food and drink are pure. One of them said: “Someone who does not know what enters his belly cannot distinguish lordly impulses from satanic ones.” Sīdī ʿAlī al-Khawwāṣ said: “Know that spiritual support continually overflows upon every human heart and takes its colour according to the heart. The heart takes its colour accordingly, in keeping with the goodness or corruption of its sustenance.” [B235] Those who eat unlawful things, such as usury and its like, rise to their dealings with the Real only as a person bereft of reason with whom Satan plays, not knowing what he says or what is said to him. He is deprived of the delight of intimate converse and the sweetness of pure dealings. If he argues in his own defence and resorts to analogy, no success is to be hoped for him on the path of the elect. Whoever receives an admonition from his Lord, desists and seeks purity has his past pardoned. Whoever returns to following the desire he had abandoned has the fire of severance as his dwelling and refuge. The Real’s way with His servants is that when someone seeks an increase in the sensory dimension of his outward being, God diminishes the light of his inward being. When someone cuts off the supply feeding this outward sensory increase, God strengthens the inflow of lights within him. “God diminishes usury and causes charities to grow”: He strengthens the support conveyed by charity’s reward. God does not love any persistent rejecter and sinner, but every obedient one who returns to Him: someone who believes with the faith of realized people and follows those granted success. Such a person is necessarily threaded into the company of divine care and taken upon the path of protecting friendship ( الولاية ): those upon whom there is no fear and who do not grieve. You who believe, be mindful of God as He should be, and leave whatever traces of sensory attachment and its causes remain within you, if you seek the faith of direct witnessing and arrival at the worshipped King. If you do not, know that you remain distant without realising it, opposing without being aware. If you return to your Lord, your principal is yours: the light of unity. You neither diminish it nor add to it—unless you direct yourselves wholly towards Him and seek arrival through Him at Him. God does not disappoint one who hopes for His generosity or turn away one who stands at His door, through His grace and bounty."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentions the debtor in hardship.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentions the debtor in hardship."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C310.1","page":310,"ar":"يقال: ما لي بهذا الأمر يدان أي: لا طاقة لي به، لأن المدافعة تكون باليد، فكأن يده معدومة لعجزه عن دفعه.","en":"One says “I have no two hands for this matter,” meaning: I have no power to deal with it. Because repelling something is done with the hand, it is as though his hand were absent, owing to his inability to repel it.","anchor":"لا يَدَانِ لنا بحرب الله ورسوله"}],"english_characters":8506,"arabic_characters":6157},{"id":"q2-280-281","q":"2:280–281","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:280–281 · Respite for the debtor and the final return","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 311–313","cairo":"I:311–313","source_page_first":311,"source_page_last":313,"ar":"وَإِنْ كانَ ذُو عُسْرَةٍ فَنَظِرَةٌ إِلى مَيْسَرَةٍ وَأَنْ تَصَدَّقُوا خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (280) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً تُرْجَعُونَ فِيهِ إِلَى اللَّهِ ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ (281)\nقلت: (كان) : تامة بمعنى حضر، وقرأ أُبَيّ وابنُ مسعود: (ذا عسرة) فتكون ناقصة، و (نظرة) : مبتدأ، والخبر محذوف، أي: فعليكم نظرة، أو فالواجب نظرة. وهو مصدر بمعنى الإنظار، وهو الإمهال، و (ميسرة) : فيه لغتان:\nالفتح والضم، وهي مَفْعَلة من اليسر، فالضم لغة أهل الحجاز، والفتح لغة تميم وقَيْس ونَجْد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وإن حضر الغريم وهو معسر، فعليكم إنظاره، أي: إمهاله إلى زمان يسره ولا يحل لكم أن تُضَيِّقُوا عليه، وتطالبوه بما ليس عنده إن أقام البيِّنَةَ على عسره وَأَنْ تَصَدَّقُوا عليه برؤوس أموالكم ولا تطالبوه بها خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ما في ذلك من الخير الجزيل والذكر الجميل.\nروى أبو هريرة رضي الله عنه أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قال: «مَنْ أَنْظَر مُعْسِراً، أو وَضَعَ عنه، أَظلَّه اللهُ في ظِلِّ عَرْشِه يوم لا ظِلَّ إِلا ظلّه» وقال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «من أَحَبَّ أن تُستجاب دعوته، وتُكشف كُربتُه، فليُيسِّرْ على المعسر» .\nوقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من أنْظَر معسراً كان له بكل يوم صدقةٌ بمثل ما أنظره به» . وقد ورد في فضل الدّين قوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «إن الله مع المدين حتى يقضي دَيْنه، ما لم يكن فيما يكره الله» . فكان عبد الله(1) يقول: «إني أكره أن أبيت ليلة إلا والله تعالى معي، فيأمر غلامه أن يأخذ بدين» .\nوقد ورد الترغيب أيضاً في الإسراع بقضاء الدين دون مطل، قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم «مَنْ مشَى إلى غَريمه بحقه، صلّت عليه دوابُّ الأرض ونُونُ الماء، وكتبت له بكل خُطوة شجرة في الجنة، وذنب يغفر له فإن لم يفعل ومطل فهو مُعْتَدِ» . وقال أيضا: «مطل الغَنِيِّ ظلم، وإذا أُتْبعَ أحدكم على مليىء فَلْيتْبَعْ» .\nثم قال تعالى: وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً تُرْجَعُونَ فِيهِ إِلَى اللَّهِ، وهو يوم القيامة، فتأهبوا للمصير إليه بالصدقة وسائر الأعمال الصالحة، ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ جزاء ما أسلفت، وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ بنقص ثواب أو تضعيف عقاب. قال ابن عباس: (هذه آخر آية نزل بها جبريل، فقال: ضعها في رأس المائتين والثمانين من البقرة، وعاش بعدها رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أحداً وعشرين يوماً) . وقيل: أحداً وثمانين، وقيل غير ذلك. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: وإن كان ذو عسرة من نور اليقين والمعرفة، فلينظر إلى أهل الغنى بالله، وليصحبهم ويتعلَّق بهم، وهم العارفون، فإنهم يغنونه بالنظر. وفي بعض الأخبار: إن لله رجالاً من نظر إليهم سعد سعادة لا يشقى بعدها أبداً. هـ. ولله رجال إذا نظروا أغنَوْا، وفي هذا المعنى يقول صاحب العينية:\nفَشَمِّرْ، ولُذْ بالأَوْليَاءِ فَإنَّهُمْ ... لَهُمْ مِنْ كِتَابِ الله تلْكَ الوقَائعُ\nهُمُ الذُّخْرُ لِلمَلهُوفِ، والكَنْزُ لِلرَّجَا ... وَمِنْهُمْ يَنَالُ الصَبُّ مَا هُوَ طامع\nوقال الشيخ أبو العباس رضي الله عنه: والله ما بيني وبين الرجل إلاَّ أن أنظر إليه وقد أغنيته. وقال فيه شيخه: نعم الرجل أبو العباس، يأتيه البدويُّ يبول على ساقه، فلا يُمسي إلا وقد أوصله إلى ربه. وقال شيخ شيوخنا سيدي العربي بن عبد الله: لو أتاني يهودي أو نصراني، لَمْ يُمْسِ إلا وقد أوصلته إلى الله. هـ. وفي كل زمان رجال يُغْنُونَ بالنظر، وقد أدركتهم، وصحبتُهم والحمد لله. والإشارة بقوله: وَأَنْ تَصَدَّقُوا خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ إلى أهل الغنى بالله، يتصدقون على الفقراء بالنظرة والهمة، حتى يحصل لهم الغنى بالله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم أمر الحق تعالى بتحصين الأموال بتقييد الديون والإشهاد عليها، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ كانَ ذُو عُسْرَةٍ فَنَظِرَةٌ إِلى مَيْسَرَةٍ وَأَنْ تَصَدَّقُوا خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (280) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً تُرْجَعُونَ فِيهِ إِلَى اللَّهِ ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ (281)","quran_en":"280. If the debtor is in hardship, grant respite until ease. To remit the debt as charity is better for you, if you know. 281. Be mindful of a day when you will be returned to God. Then every soul will be paid in full for what it earned, and they will not be wronged.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The complete and incomplete verb kāna","html":"<p>I say: <i>kāna<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كان<\/bdi>) is complete (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تامة<\/bdi>), meaning “be present.” Ubayy and Ibn Masʿūd read “one in hardship” in the accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذا عسرة<\/bdi>), making <i>kāna<\/i> incomplete (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ناقصة<\/bdi>). “A respite” is a subject whose predicate is omitted: “A respite is incumbent upon you,” or “What is obligatory is a respite.” It is a verbal noun meaning granting delay. <i>Maysara<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ميسرة<\/bdi>) has two forms, with <i>fatḥa<\/i> or <i>ḍamma<\/i> on the <i>sīn<\/i>. It is formed from ease: <i>ḍamma<\/i> is the usage of the Ḥijāz, and <i>fatḥa<\/i> that of Tamīm, Qays and Najd.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: kāna ( كان ) is complete ( تامة ), meaning “be present.” Ubayy and Ibn Masʿūd read “one in hardship” in the accusative ( ذا عسرة ), making kāna incomplete ( ناقصة ). “A respite” is a subject whose predicate is omitted: “A respite is incumbent upon you,” or “What is obligatory is a respite.” It is a verbal noun meaning granting delay. Maysara ( ميسرة ) has two forms, with fatḥa or ḍamma on the sīn . It is formed from ease: ḍamma is the usage of the Ḥijāz, and fatḥa that of Tamīm, Qays and Najd."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Give time, or forgive the principal","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: if the debtor appears in hardship, you must grant him time until he has means. It is not lawful to constrain him or demand what he does not possess if he establishes his hardship by evidence. Giving him your principal as charity, without demanding it, is better for you, if you know the abundant good and beautiful remembrance that this brings.<\/p><p>Abū Hurayra related that the Prophet said: “Whoever grants respite to someone in hardship or remits his debt, God shades him beneath His Throne on the day when there is no shade except His shade.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever wishes his prayer answered and distress relieved should make things easy for someone in hardship”; and, “Whoever grants respite to a debtor in hardship has, for every day, a charity equal to the sum he deferred.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-270\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B236]<\/a><\/p><p>Concerning debt, it is transmitted that the Prophet said: “God is with the debtor until he discharges his debt, provided it is not for something God dislikes.” ʿAbd Allāh would say: “I dislike passing a night without God Most High being with me,” and would instruct his servant to purchase on credit.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-271\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B236]<\/a><\/p><p>Prompt repayment without delay is also encouraged. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever walks to his creditor with what is due has the animals of the earth and fish of the water pray for him. For every step, a tree in the Garden is recorded for him and a sin forgiven. If he does not do so but delays, he is a transgressor.” He also said: “Delay by someone with means is wrongdoing. If one of you is referred to a solvent debtor, let him accept the transfer.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-272\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B236]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: if the debtor appears in hardship, you must grant him time until he has means. It is not lawful to constrain him or demand what he does not possess if he establishes his hardship by evidence. Giving him your principal as charity, without demanding it, is better for you, if you know the abundant good and beautiful remembrance that this brings. Abū Hurayra related that the Prophet said: “Whoever grants respite to someone in hardship or remits his debt, God shades him beneath His Throne on the day when there is no shade except His shade.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever wishes his prayer answered and distress relieved should make things easy for someone in hardship”; and, “Whoever grants respite to a debtor in hardship has, for every day, a charity equal to the sum he deferred.” [B236] Concerning debt, it is transmitted that the Prophet said: “God is with the debtor until he discharges his debt, provided it is not for something God dislikes.” ʿAbd Allāh would say: “I dislike passing a night without God Most High being with me,” and would instruct his servant to purchase on credit. [B236] Prompt repayment without delay is also encouraged. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever walks to his creditor with what is due has the animals of the earth and fish of the water pray for him. For every step, a tree in the Garden is recorded for him and a sin forgiven. If he does not do so but delays, he is a transgressor.” He also said: “Delay by someone with means is wrongdoing. If one of you is referred to a solvent debtor, let him accept the transfer.” [B236]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The final accounting","html":"<p>“Be mindful of a day when you will be returned to God”: the Day of Resurrection. Prepare for your return through charity and all other good deeds. Every soul will then receive the full recompense of what it sent ahead, without diminished reward or increased punishment.<\/p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās said: “This was the last verse Gabriel brought down. He said, ‘Place it after the first two hundred and eighty verses of al-Baqara.’ The Messenger of God lived twenty-one days after it.” Other accounts give eighty-one days or other intervals. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"note-ref\" href=\"#/note/XVI-N003\">[XVI-N003]<\/a><a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-273\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B237]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“Be mindful of a day when you will be returned to God”: the Day of Resurrection. Prepare for your return through charity and all other good deeds. Every soul will then receive the full recompense of what it sent ahead, without diminished reward or increased punishment. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “This was the last verse Gabriel brought down. He said, ‘Place it after the first two hundred and eighty verses of al-Baqara.’ The Messenger of God lived twenty-one days after it.” Other accounts give eighty-one days or other intervals. And God Most High knows best. [XVI-N003] [B237]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The poverty that is relieved by a glance","html":"<p>If someone is poor in the light of certainty and gnosis, let him look towards those rich through God, accompany them and attach himself to them: the gnostics, who enrich through a glance. A transmitted saying declares that God has men whom someone need only see to receive a happiness never followed by wretchedness. God has men who enrich by looking. The author of the poem rhyming in <i>ʿayn<\/i> says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Make ready and seek refuge with the saints:<br>theirs are those encounters from God’s Book.<br>They are provision for the distressed, a treasure for hope;<br>from them the ardent lover gains what he desires.<\/blockquote><p>Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās said: “By God, between me and a man there is only my looking at him, and I have enriched him.” His shaykh said of him: “What an excellent man is Abū al-ʿAbbās! A rustic who urinates upon his own leg comes to him, and before evening he has brought him to his Lord.”<\/p><p>Our teachers’ teacher, Sīdī al-ʿArabī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, said: “Were a Jew or Christian to come to me, evening would not fall before I had brought him to God.” In every age there are men who enrich through a glance. I have encountered and accompanied them, and praise belongs to God.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-274\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B238]<\/a><\/p><p>“To remit as charity is better for you” addresses those rich through God: let them give to the poor through their glance and aspiration until the latter attain richness through God. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If someone is poor in the light of certainty and gnosis, let him look towards those rich through God, accompany them and attach himself to them: the gnostics, who enrich through a glance. A transmitted saying declares that God has men whom someone need only see to receive a happiness never followed by wretchedness. God has men who enrich by looking. The author of the poem rhyming in ʿayn says: Make ready and seek refuge with the saints: theirs are those encounters from God’s Book. They are provision for the distressed, a treasure for hope; from them the ardent lover gains what he desires. Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās said: “By God, between me and a man there is only my looking at him, and I have enriched him.” His shaykh said of him: “What an excellent man is Abū al-ʿAbbās! A rustic who urinates upon his own leg comes to him, and before evening he has brought him to his Lord.” Our teachers’ teacher, Sīdī al-ʿArabī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, said: “Were a Jew or Christian to come to me, evening would not fall before I had brought him to God.” In every age there are men who enrich through a glance. I have encountered and accompanied them, and praise belongs to God. [B238] “To remit as charity is better for you” addresses those rich through God: let them give to the poor through their glance and aspiration until the latter attain richness through God. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then commands the safeguarding of wealth by recording debts and having them witnessed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then commands the safeguarding of wealth by recording debts and having them witnessed."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C312.1","page":312,"ar":"وهو عبد الله بن جعفر بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنهما.","en":"He is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib, may God be pleased with them both.","anchor":"فكان عبد الله"}],"english_characters":4172,"arabic_characters":3151},{"id":"q2-282","q":"2:282","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:282 · Recording debts, truthful testimony and the gift of knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 313–315","cairo":"I:313–315","source_page_first":313,"source_page_last":315,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذا تَدايَنْتُمْ بِدَيْنٍ إِلى أَجَلٍ مُسَمًّى فَاكْتُبُوهُ وَلْيَكْتُبْ بَيْنَكُمْ كاتِبٌ بِالْعَدْلِ وَلا يَأْبَ كاتِبٌ أَنْ يَكْتُبَ كَما عَلَّمَهُ اللَّهُ فَلْيَكْتُبْ وَلْيُمْلِلِ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ وَلا يَبْخَسْ مِنْهُ شَيْئاً فَإِنْ كانَ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ سَفِيهاً أَوْ ضَعِيفاً أَوْ لا يَسْتَطِيعُ أَنْ يُمِلَّ هُوَ فَلْيُمْلِلْ وَلِيُّهُ بِالْعَدْلِ وَاسْتَشْهِدُوا شَهِيدَيْنِ مِنْ رِجالِكُمْ فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُونا رَجُلَيْنِ فَرَجُلٌ وَامْرَأَتانِ مِمَّنْ تَرْضَوْنَ مِنَ الشُّهَداءِ أَنْ تَضِلَّ إِحْداهُما فَتُذَكِّرَ إِحْداهُمَا الْأُخْرى ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذا تَدايَنْتُمْ بِدَيْنٍ أي: داين بعضكم بعضاً فى بيع أو سلف، إِلى أَجَلٍ مُسَمًّى أي: معلوم بالأيام أو الأشهر، لا بالحصاد أو قدوم الحاج، إلا في السَّلمَ، فَاكْتُبُوهُ لأنه أوثق وأدفع للنزاع. والجمهور: أن الأمر للاستحباب، وَلْيَكْتُبْ بَيْنَكُمْ كاتِبٌ بِالْعَدْلِ لا يزيد ولا ينقص، ولا بد أن يكون عدلا حتى يجيىء مكتوبه موثوقاً به، وَلا يَأْبَ كاتِبٌ أَنْ يَكْتُبَ أي: ولا يمتنع كاتب من الكتابة كَما عَلَّمَهُ اللَّهُ فَلْيَكْتُبْ أي: فليكتب كما علمه الله من كتابة الوثائق، أو: لا يأب أن ينفع الناس بكتابته كما نفعه الله بتعليمها. وَلْيُمْلِلِ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ أي: وليكن المُملي مَنْ عليه الحق لأنه المُقر للشهود، يقال: أملل وأملى، إذا ذكر ما عنده أو ما عليه، وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ أي: المملي أو الكاتب، وَلا يَبْخَسْ مِنْهُ شَيْئاً أي: ولا ينقص من الحق الذي عليه شيئاً في الإملاء أو في الكتابة.\nفَإِنْ كانَ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ سَفِيهاً: ناقص العقل مُبذراً، أَوْ ضَعِيفاً شيخاً مخبلاً، أو صبيّاً صغيراً، أَوْ لا يَسْتَطِيعُ أَنْ يُمِلَّ هُوَ، لخرس أو جهل باللغة، فَلْيُمْلِلْ عنه وَلِيُّهُ بِالْعَدْلِ، من وَصِيٍّ أو وكيل، وَاسْتَشْهِدُوا على معاملتكم شَهِيدَيْنِ مِنْ رِجالِكُمْ المسلمين، فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُونا رَجُلَيْنِ، بأن تعذر إحضارهما، فَرَجُلٌ وَامْرَأَتانِ فأكثر، تقوم مقام رجلين مِمَّنْ تَرْضَوْنَ مِنَ الشُّهَداءِ لعلمكم بعدالتهم، وإنما شرط تعدد النساء لأجل أَنْ تَضِلَّ إِحْداهُما فَتُذَكِّرَ إِحْداهُمَا الْأُخْرى أي: إن ضلت إحداهما الشهادة، ونسيتها، ذكرتها الأخرى لأنها ناقصة عقل ودين.\nثم حذَّر الشهود من الامتناع عن تحمل الشهادة أو أدائها، فقال:\n... وَلا يَأْبَ الشُّهَداءُ إِذا ما دُعُوا وَلا تَسْئَمُوا أَنْ تَكْتُبُوهُ صَغِيراً أَوْ كَبِيراً إِلى أَجَلِهِ ذلِكُمْ أَقْسَطُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَأَقْوَمُ لِلشَّهادَةِ وَأَدْنى أَلاَّ تَرْتابُوا إِلاَّ أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةً حاضِرَةً تُدِيرُونَها بَيْنَكُمْ فَلَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَلاَّ تَكْتُبُوها وَأَشْهِدُوا إِذا تَبايَعْتُمْ وَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ وَإِنْ تَفْعَلُوا فَإِنَّهُ فُسُوقٌ بِكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَيُعَلِّمُكُمُ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ (282)\nقلت: السَّأَمُ هو: الملَلُ، و (لا يضار) يحتمل أن يكون مبنيّاً للفاعل، وأصله: يضارر بالكسر، أو للمفعول، فيكون الأصح بالفتح.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولا يمتنع الشُّهَداءِ من تحمل الشهادة إذا دعوا إليها، حيث تعيَّنت عليهم، وسُموا شهداء باعتبار المآل، وإنما تتعين إذا لم يوجد غيرهم. أو: من أدائها حيث لا ضرر، وَلا تَسْئَمُوا أَنْ تَكْتُبُوهُ أي: ولا تملوا من كتابة الحق إذا تكرر صَغِيراً كان أَوْ كَبِيراً، فقيدوا ذلك إِلى أَجَلِهِ، ذلِكُمْ أَقْسَطُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ أي: ذلك الكتاب والتقييد للحقوق، أكثر قسطاً عند الله لأنه أدفع للنزاع وأحفظ للحقوق، وَأَقْوَمُ لِلشَّهادَةِ أي: أثبتُ لها وأعون على أدائها، وَأَدْنى أَلَّا تَرْتابُوا أي: وأقرب لعدم الريب والشك في جنس الدين وقَدْره وأجلَه، لأنه إذا كتب جنسه وقدره وأجله لم يبق لأحد شك في ذلك، إِلَّا أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةً حاضِرَةً لا أجَل فيها، تُدِيرُونَها بَيْنَكُمْ أي: تتعاملون فيها نقداً، فَلَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَلَّا تَكْتُبُوها لقلة النزاع فيها، وَأَشْهِدُوا إِذا تَبايَعْتُمْ مطلقاً بدين أو نقد لأنه أحوط، خوفاً من الإنكار، والأوامر في هذه الآية للاستحباب عند الأكثر.\nوَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ بالتحريف والتغيير في الكتابة والشهادة، على البناء للفاعل، أو: ولا يضارا بأن يُعْجلا عن مهم، أو يكلفا الأداء من شقة بعيدة، أو يمنع من أجرته، وَإِنْ تَفْعَلُوا ذلك الضرار وما نهيتهم عنه فَإِنَّهُ فُسُوقٌ بِكُمْ أي: خروج بكم عن حد الاستقامة، وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ في مخالفة أمره ونهيه، وَيُعَلِّمُكُمُ اللَّهُ العلوم اللدنية وَاللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ فلا يخفى عليه من اتقى الله ممن عصاه. وكرر لفظ الجلالة في الجمل الثلاث، لاستقلالها، فإن الأُولى حثٌّ على التقوى والثانية وعدٌ بتعليم العلم، والثالثة تعظيم لشأنه، ولأنه أدخل في التعظيم من الكناية. قاله البيضاوي.\nوأدخل الواو فى جواب الأمر ليقتضي أن تعليمه سبحانه لأهل التقوى ليس هو مسبباً عن التقوى، بل هو بمحض الفضل والكرم، والتقوى إنما هي طريق موصل لذلك الكرم، لا سبب فيه «جَلَّ حكم الأزل أن يضاف إلى العلل» . والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى حكم الرهان، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذا تَدايَنْتُمْ بِدَيْنٍ إِلى أَجَلٍ مُسَمًّى فَاكْتُبُوهُ وَلْيَكْتُبْ بَيْنَكُمْ كاتِبٌ بِالْعَدْلِ وَلا يَأْبَ كاتِبٌ أَنْ يَكْتُبَ كَما عَلَّمَهُ اللَّهُ فَلْيَكْتُبْ وَلْيُمْلِلِ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ وَلا يَبْخَسْ مِنْهُ شَيْئاً فَإِنْ كانَ الَّذِي عَلَيْهِ الْحَقُّ سَفِيهاً أَوْ ضَعِيفاً أَوْ لا يَسْتَطِيعُ أَنْ يُمِلَّ هُوَ فَلْيُمْلِلْ وَلِيُّهُ بِالْعَدْلِ وَاسْتَشْهِدُوا شَهِيدَيْنِ مِنْ رِجالِكُمْ فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُونا رَجُلَيْنِ فَرَجُلٌ وَامْرَأَتانِ مِمَّنْ تَرْضَوْنَ مِنَ الشُّهَداءِ أَنْ تَضِلَّ إِحْداهُما فَتُذَكِّرَ إِحْداهُمَا الْأُخْرى وَلا يَأْبَ الشُّهَداءُ إِذا ما دُعُوا وَلا تَسْئَمُوا أَنْ تَكْتُبُوهُ صَغِيراً أَوْ كَبِيراً إِلى أَجَلِهِ ذلِكُمْ أَقْسَطُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَأَقْوَمُ لِلشَّهادَةِ وَأَدْنى أَلاَّ تَرْتابُوا إِلاَّ أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةً حاضِرَةً تُدِيرُونَها بَيْنَكُمْ فَلَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَلاَّ تَكْتُبُوها وَأَشْهِدُوا إِذا تَبايَعْتُمْ وَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ وَإِنْ تَفْعَلُوا فَإِنَّهُ فُسُوقٌ بِكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَيُعَلِّمُكُمُ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ (282)","quran_en":"282. You who believe, when you contract a debt with one another for a specified term, write it down. Let a scribe write between you justly. No scribe should refuse to write as God has taught him; let him write, and let the debtor dictate. Let him be mindful of God, his Lord, and diminish nothing from it. If the debtor lacks sound judgment, is weak or cannot dictate himself, let his guardian dictate justly. Call two witnesses from among your men. If there are not two men, then one man and two women from witnesses you approve, so that if one of the two errs, the other may remind her. Witnesses should not refuse when called. Do not tire of writing the debt down, small or great, with its term. That is more equitable before God, firmer for testimony and nearer to preventing doubt—unless it is immediate trade you conduct among yourselves; then there is no blame upon you for not writing it down. Take witnesses when you trade. No scribe or witness shall be harmed. If you do that, it is disobedience on your part. Be mindful of God; God teaches you; and God knows all things.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The term, the record and the debtor’s dictation","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, when you contract a debt with one another through sale or loan, for a term specified by days or months—not by harvest or the pilgrims’ return, except in a forward purchase (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السَّلَم<\/bdi>)—write it down. This is more secure and more effective in preventing dispute. The majority understand the command as recommendation.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-275\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B239]<\/a><\/p><p>Let a scribe write between you justly, adding and omitting nothing. He must be upright (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عدل<\/bdi>), so that his document is trustworthy. No scribe should refuse to write as God has taught him. Let him write in accordance with what God has taught him about recording contracts; or let him not refuse to benefit people through writing as God benefited him by teaching it.<\/p><p>Let the debtor dictate, since he is the one acknowledging the obligation before the witnesses. <i>Amlala<\/i> and <i>amlā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أملل، أملى<\/bdi>) both mean setting out what one holds or owes. Let him—the dictator or scribe—be mindful of God, his Lord, and diminish nothing of the debt in dictation or writing.<\/p><p>If the debtor lacks sound judgment and is wasteful, or is weak—an elderly man whose mind has become impaired, or a young child—or cannot dictate because he cannot speak or does not know the language, let his guardian, testamentary guardian or agent dictate justly on his behalf.<\/p><p>Call two Muslim men to witness the transaction. If two men cannot be brought, one man and two women or more take their place, from witnesses whose uprightness you approve. The plurality of women is stipulated so that if one loses track of the testimony and forgets, the other reminds her, since she is described here as deficient in intellect and religion.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-276\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B239]<\/a><\/p><p>He then warns witnesses against refusing to undertake or give testimony.<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:282b\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:282b<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَلا يَأْبَ الشُّهَداءُ إِذا ما دُعُوا وَلا تَسْئَمُوا أَنْ تَكْتُبُوهُ صَغِيراً أَوْ كَبِيراً إِلى أَجَلِهِ ذلِكُمْ أَقْسَطُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَأَقْوَمُ لِلشَّهادَةِ وَأَدْنى أَلَّا تَرْتابُوا إِلَّا أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةً حاضِرَةً تُدِيرُونَها بَيْنَكُمْ فَلَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَلَّا تَكْتُبُوها وَأَشْهِدُوا إِذا تَبايَعْتُمْ وَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ وَإِنْ تَفْعَلُوا فَإِنَّهُ فُسُوقٌ بِكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَيُعَلِّمُكُمُ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">Witnesses should not refuse when called. Do not tire of writing the debt down, small or great, with its term. That is more equitable before God, firmer for testimony and nearer to preventing doubt—unless it is immediate trade you conduct among yourselves; then there is no blame upon you for not writing it down. Take witnesses when you trade. No scribe or witness shall be harmed. If you do that, it is disobedience on your part. Be mindful of God; God teaches you; and God knows all things.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, when you contract a debt with one another through sale or loan, for a term specified by days or months—not by harvest or the pilgrims’ return, except in a forward purchase ( السَّلَم )—write it down. This is more secure and more effective in preventing dispute. The majority understand the command as recommendation. [B239] Let a scribe write between you justly, adding and omitting nothing. He must be upright ( عدل ), so that his document is trustworthy. No scribe should refuse to write as God has taught him. Let him write in accordance with what God has taught him about recording contracts; or let him not refuse to benefit people through writing as God benefited him by teaching it. Let the debtor dictate, since he is the one acknowledging the obligation before the witnesses. Amlala and amlā ( أملل، أملى ) both mean setting out what one holds or owes. Let him—the dictator or scribe—be mindful of God, his Lord, and diminish nothing of the debt in dictation or writing. If the debtor lacks sound judgment and is wasteful, or is weak—an elderly man whose mind has become impaired, or a young child—or cannot dictate because he cannot speak or does not know the language, let his guardian, testamentary guardian or agent dictate justly on his behalf. Call two Muslim men to witness the transaction. If two men cannot be brought, one man and two women or more take their place, from witnesses whose uprightness you approve. The plurality of women is stipulated so that if one loses track of the testimony and forgets, the other reminds her, since she is described here as deficient in intellect and religion. [B239] He then warns witnesses against refusing to undertake or give testimony. Qurʾānic continuation · 2:282b وَلا يَأْبَ الشُّهَداءُ إِذا ما دُعُوا وَلا تَسْئَمُوا أَنْ تَكْتُبُوهُ صَغِيراً أَوْ كَبِيراً إِلى أَجَلِهِ ذلِكُمْ أَقْسَطُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَأَقْوَمُ لِلشَّهادَةِ وَأَدْنى أَلَّا تَرْتابُوا إِلَّا أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةً حاضِرَةً تُدِيرُونَها بَيْنَكُمْ فَلَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَلَّا تَكْتُبُوها وَأَشْهِدُوا إِذا تَبايَعْتُمْ وَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ وَإِنْ تَفْعَلُوا فَإِنَّهُ فُسُوقٌ بِكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَيُعَلِّمُكُمُ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيمٌ Witnesses should not refuse when called. Do not tire of writing the debt down, small or great, with its term. That is more equitable before God, firmer for testimony and nearer to preventing doubt—unless it is immediate trade you conduct among yourselves; then there is no blame upon you for not writing it down. Take witnesses when you trade. No scribe or witness shall be harmed. If you do that, it is disobedience on your part. Be mindful of God; God teaches you; and God knows all things."},{"type":"language","title":"Weariness and the active or passive verb","html":"<p>I say: <i>saʾam<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السأم<\/bdi>) means weariness. “Shall not harm or be harmed” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا يضار<\/bdi>) may be active, originally <i>yuḍārir<\/i> with <i>kasra<\/i>, or passive, with <i>fatḥa<\/i> in the original unassimilated form.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-277\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B240]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: saʾam ( السأم ) means weariness. “Shall not harm or be harmed” ( لا يضار ) may be active, originally yuḍārir with kasra , or passive, with fatḥa in the original unassimilated form. [B240]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Witnesses, immediate trade and the prevention of injury","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: witnesses must not refuse to undertake testimony when called, where the duty falls specifically upon them. They are called witnesses in view of what they will become. The duty becomes individual only if no others are available. Or the prohibition concerns refusing to deliver testimony where no harm is involved.<\/p><p>Do not tire of writing down an obligation when it recurs, whether small or great. Record it with its term. Such recording is more equitable before God, since it better prevents dispute and safeguards rights; firmer for testimony, making it more established and easier to deliver; and nearer to preventing doubt about the debt’s kind, amount and term. Once these are written, no one remains uncertain about them.<\/p><p>An exception is immediate trade, with no deferred term, conducted for ready payment among yourselves. There is no blame for not writing it because dispute is less likely. Take witnesses whenever you trade, whether on credit or for cash, as a precaution against denial. The majority regard the commands in this verse as recommendations.<\/p><p>On the active construction, neither scribe nor witness should harm another by distorting or altering the document or testimony. On the passive construction, neither should be harmed by being hurried away from an important affair, required to give testimony from a far-off place or denied his fee. If you commit such injury and what you have been forbidden, it is departure from uprightness on your part.<\/p><p>Be mindful of God by avoiding opposition to His commands and prohibitions. God teaches you the divinely bestowed sciences. God knows all things: the reverent and the disobedient alike are not hidden from Him.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: witnesses must not refuse to undertake testimony when called, where the duty falls specifically upon them. They are called witnesses in view of what they will become. The duty becomes individual only if no others are available. Or the prohibition concerns refusing to deliver testimony where no harm is involved. Do not tire of writing down an obligation when it recurs, whether small or great. Record it with its term. Such recording is more equitable before God, since it better prevents dispute and safeguards rights; firmer for testimony, making it more established and easier to deliver; and nearer to preventing doubt about the debt’s kind, amount and term. Once these are written, no one remains uncertain about them. An exception is immediate trade, with no deferred term, conducted for ready payment among yourselves. There is no blame for not writing it because dispute is less likely. Take witnesses whenever you trade, whether on credit or for cash, as a precaution against denial. The majority regard the commands in this verse as recommendations. On the active construction, neither scribe nor witness should harm another by distorting or altering the document or testimony. On the passive construction, neither should be harmed by being hurried away from an important affair, required to give testimony from a far-off place or denied his fee. If you commit such injury and what you have been forbidden, it is departure from uprightness on your part. Be mindful of God by avoiding opposition to His commands and prohibitions. God teaches you the divinely bestowed sciences. God knows all things: the reverent and the disobedient alike are not hidden from Him."},{"type":"language","title":"Teaching is a gift, not an effect produced by reverence","html":"<p>The divine Name is repeated in the three clauses because each is independent: the first urges reverence; the second promises teaching; the third magnifies His standing. Explicit naming also conveys greater magnification than a pronoun. Al-Bayḍāwī says this.<\/p><p>The <i>wāw<\/i> is introduced in the response to the command to show that God’s teaching of the reverent is not an effect caused by their reverence, but pure bounty and generosity. Reverence is a path leading to that generosity, not a cause producing it. “The judgment of eternity is too exalted to be attributed to causes.” And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-278\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B240]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The divine Name is repeated in the three clauses because each is independent: the first urges reverence; the second promises teaching; the third magnifies His standing. Explicit naming also conveys greater magnification than a pronoun. Al-Bayḍāwī says this. The wāw is introduced in the response to the command to show that God’s teaching of the reverent is not an effect caused by their reverence, but pure bounty and generosity. Reverence is a path leading to that generosity, not a cause producing it. “The judgment of eternity is too exalted to be attributed to causes.” And God Most High knows best. [B240]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then mentions the ruling on pledges.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then mentions the ruling on pledges."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5325,"arabic_characters":4524},{"id":"q2-283","q":"2:283","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:283 · Pledges, trust and recording the gifts of the heart","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 315–316","cairo":"I:315–316","source_page_first":315,"source_page_last":316,"ar":"وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ عَلى سَفَرٍ وَلَمْ تَجِدُوا كاتِباً فَرِهانٌ مَقْبُوضَةٌ فَإِنْ أَمِنَ بَعْضُكُمْ بَعْضاً فَلْيُؤَدِّ الَّذِي اؤْتُمِنَ أَمانَتَهُ وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ وَلا تَكْتُمُوا الشَّهادَةَ وَمَنْ يَكْتُمْها فَإِنَّهُ آثِمٌ قَلْبُهُ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ عَلِيمٌ (283)\nقلت: (فرهان) : خبر، أو مبتدأ، أي: فالمستوثق به رهان، أو فعليه رهان.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ عَلى جناح سَفَرٍ أي: مسافرين، وَلَمْ تَجِدُوا كاتِباً يكتب شهادة البيع أو الدين، فالمستوثق به عوضا من الإشهاد: فَرِهانٌ مَقْبُوضَةٌ. وليس السفر شرطاً فى صحة الارتهان، لأنه عليه الصلاة والسلام «رَهَنَ دِرْعَه عند يهودي بالمدينة في شَعِير» لكنّ لمّا كان السفر مَظنَّة إعواز الكتاب، ذكره الحق تعالى حكماً للغالب. والجمهور على اعتبار القبض فيه، فإن لم يقبض حتى حصل المانع، فلا يختص به في دينه، فَإِنْ أَمِنَ بَعْضُكُمْ بَعْضاً واستغنى بأمانته عن الارتهان، لوثوقه بأمانته فداينه بلا رهن، فَلْيُؤَدِّ الَّذِي اؤْتُمِنَ أَمانَتَهُ أي: دينه، وسماه أمانة لائتمانه عليه بلا ارتهان ولا إشهاد، وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ في أداء دينه وعدم إنكاره.\nوَلا تَكْتُمُوا الشَّهادَةَ أيها الشهود، أو أهل الدين، أي: شهادتهم على أنفسهم، وَمَنْ يَكْتُمْها منكم بأن يمتنع من أداء ما تحمل من الشهادة، أو من أداء ما عليه من الدين، فَإِنَّهُ آثِمٌ قَلْبُهُ حيث كتم ما علمه به، لأن الكتمان من عمل القلوب فتعلق الإثم به، ونظيره: «العين زانية وزناها النظر» ، أو أسنده إلى القلب، مبالغة لأنه رئيس الأعضاء، فإذا أثم قلبه فقد أثم كله، وكأنه قد تمكن الإثم منه فأخذ أشرف أجزائه، وفاق سائر ذنوبه، ثم هدد\nالكاتمين فقال: وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ عَلِيمٌ لا يخفى عليه ما تبدون وما تكتمون، روى عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «من كَتَمَ شهادةً إذا دُعِي- كَان كَمَنْ شَهِدَ بالزُّور» .\nالإشارة: كما أمر الله تعالى بتقييد الديون الدنيوية، والاعتناء بشأنها، أمر بتقييد العلوم اللدنية والواردات القدسية والاغتباط بأمرها، بل هي أولى لدوام ثمراتها وخلود نتائجها، فإن الحكمة ترد على القلب من عالم القدس عظيمة كالجبل، فإن أهملتها ولم تبادر إلى تقييدها، رجعت كالجمل، فإن أخرتها رجعت كالطير، ثم كالبيضة، ثم تمتحي من القلب، وفي هذا المعنى قيل:\nالعلمُ صيدٌ والكتابةُ قَيْدُه ... قَيِّدْ صُيودَك بالحِبَالِ المُوثِقَهْ\nوَمِنَ الجَهَالة أن تصِيدَ حمامةً ... وتتركُها بَيْنَ الأوانِس مطلقهْ\nفإن لم يحسن الكتابة، فليملله على من يُحسنها، ولا يبخس منه شيئاً، بل يمُليه على ما ورد في قلبه، فإن كان ضعيف العبارة، فليملل عنه من يحسنها بالعدل، من غير زيادة ولا نقصان في المعنى، وليُشهد عليها رجال أهل الفن وهم العارفون، فإن لم يكونوا، فمن حضر من الفقراء المتمكنين لئلا يكون في تلك الحكمة شيء من الخلل لنقصان صاحبها، أو: وليُشْهِد على ذلك الوارد عدلين، وهما الكتاب والسنّة، فإن كان موافقاً لهما، قُبل، وإلا ردّ.\nقال الجنيد رضي الله عنه: إن النكتةَ لتقع في قلبي فلا أقبلها إلا بشهادة عَدْلَين: الكتاب والسنّة. هـ. وإن كنتم مستعجلين، ولم تجدوا كاتباً، فارتهنوها في قلوب بعضكم بعضاً، حتى تُقيد. ومن كتم الواردات عن شيخه أو إخوانه، فقد أثم قلبه لأنه نوع من الخيانة في طريق التربية. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم هدّد الحق تعالى عباده، على مخالفة ما أمرهم به، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ عَلى سَفَرٍ وَلَمْ تَجِدُوا كاتِباً فَرِهانٌ مَقْبُوضَةٌ فَإِنْ أَمِنَ بَعْضُكُمْ بَعْضاً فَلْيُؤَدِّ الَّذِي اؤْتُمِنَ أَمانَتَهُ وَلْيَتَّقِ اللَّهَ رَبَّهُ وَلا تَكْتُمُوا الشَّهادَةَ وَمَنْ يَكْتُمْها فَإِنَّهُ آثِمٌ قَلْبُهُ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ عَلِيمٌ (283)","quran_en":"283. If you are travelling and find no scribe, let there be a pledge taken into possession. If one of you trusts another, let the one entrusted discharge his trust and be mindful of God, his Lord. Do not conceal testimony. Whoever conceals it has a sinful heart. God knows what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The implied predicate or subject","html":"<p>I say: “A pledge” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رهان<\/bdi>) may be predicate or subject: “The security is a pledge,” or “A pledge is incumbent upon him.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “A pledge” ( رهان ) may be predicate or subject: “The security is a pledge,” or “A pledge is incumbent upon him.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Possession of security and honouring trust","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: if you are travelling and find no scribe to record testimony to a sale or debt, the security replacing formal witnessing is a pledge taken into possession. Travel is not a condition of a valid pledge: the Prophet “pledged his coat of mail with a Jew in Medina for barley.” Travel is mentioned as the usual circumstance in which scribes are unavailable.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-279\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B241]<\/a><\/p><p>The majority require taking possession. If this has not occurred before an impediment arises, the creditor has no exclusive claim to the pledged asset against his debt. If one of you trusts another and dispenses with a pledge because he trusts his honesty, lending without security, let the one entrusted discharge his trust—that is, his debt. It is called a trust because he was trusted without pledge or witnesses. Let him be mindful of God, his Lord, by repaying and not denying the debt.<\/p><p>Do not conceal testimony, witnesses—or debtors, concerning their acknowledgment against themselves. Whoever refuses to give the testimony he undertook, or to pay the debt he owes, has a sinful heart, concealing what it knows. Concealment is an act of hearts, so the sin is attached to the heart. A parallel is: “The eye commits fornication, and its fornication is looking.” Alternatively, the sin is ascribed to the heart for emphasis, since it is chief among the limbs. If the heart sins, the whole person sins: the sin has taken firm hold of his noblest part and surpassed his other sins.<\/p><p>He then threatens those who conceal:<\/p><p>“God knows what you do.” Neither what you reveal nor what you hide is concealed from Him. It is related that the Prophet said: “Whoever conceals testimony when called is like someone who gives false testimony.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-280\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B241]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: if you are travelling and find no scribe to record testimony to a sale or debt, the security replacing formal witnessing is a pledge taken into possession. Travel is not a condition of a valid pledge: the Prophet “pledged his coat of mail with a Jew in Medina for barley.” Travel is mentioned as the usual circumstance in which scribes are unavailable. [B241] The majority require taking possession. If this has not occurred before an impediment arises, the creditor has no exclusive claim to the pledged asset against his debt. If one of you trusts another and dispenses with a pledge because he trusts his honesty, lending without security, let the one entrusted discharge his trust—that is, his debt. It is called a trust because he was trusted without pledge or witnesses. Let him be mindful of God, his Lord, by repaying and not denying the debt. Do not conceal testimony, witnesses—or debtors, concerning their acknowledgment against themselves. Whoever refuses to give the testimony he undertook, or to pay the debt he owes, has a sinful heart, concealing what it knows. Concealment is an act of hearts, so the sin is attached to the heart. A parallel is: “The eye commits fornication, and its fornication is looking.” Alternatively, the sin is ascribed to the heart for emphasis, since it is chief among the limbs. If the heart sins, the whole person sins: the sin has taken firm hold of his noblest part and surpassed his other sins. He then threatens those who conceal: “God knows what you do.” Neither what you reveal nor what you hide is concealed from Him. It is related that the Prophet said: “Whoever conceals testimony when called is like someone who gives false testimony.” [B241]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Record inspiration, then submit it to its witnesses","html":"<p>As God commands the recording of worldly debts and care for them, He commands the recording of divinely bestowed sciences and sacred incoming inspirations, and rejoicing in them. These deserve it even more, for their fruits endure and their results abide.<\/p><p>A wisdom may come to the heart from the world of holiness, immense as a mountain. If you neglect it and do not promptly record it, it shrinks to the size of a camel. Delay further and it becomes like a bird, then an egg, then is erased from the heart. In this sense it is said:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Knowledge is game, and writing its tether;<br>bind your catches with strong cords.<br>It is ignorance to catch a dove<br>and leave it loose among its companions.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-281\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B242]<\/a><\/blockquote><p>Someone unable to write should dictate it to someone who can, diminishing nothing but dictating as it came to his heart. If his expression is weak, let someone who expresses himself well dictate justly on his behalf, without adding or subtracting meaning. Let him call men skilled in this art—the gnostics—to witness it; failing them, whoever is present among firmly established spiritual poor, lest the wisdom contain a defect arising from its recipient’s deficiency.<\/p><p>Or let him call two upright witnesses to the incoming inspiration: the Book and the Sunna. If it accords with them, it is accepted; otherwise it is rejected. Al-Junayd said: “A subtle point may fall into my heart, but I do not accept it without the testimony of two upright witnesses: the Book and the Sunna.”<\/p><p>If you are in haste and find no scribe, entrust these inspirations as pledges within one another’s hearts until they can be recorded. Whoever conceals incoming inspirations from his shaykh or brothers has a sinful heart, for that is a kind of betrayal on the path of spiritual nurture. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"As God commands the recording of worldly debts and care for them, He commands the recording of divinely bestowed sciences and sacred incoming inspirations, and rejoicing in them. These deserve it even more, for their fruits endure and their results abide. A wisdom may come to the heart from the world of holiness, immense as a mountain. If you neglect it and do not promptly record it, it shrinks to the size of a camel. Delay further and it becomes like a bird, then an egg, then is erased from the heart. In this sense it is said: Knowledge is game, and writing its tether; bind your catches with strong cords. It is ignorance to catch a dove and leave it loose among its companions. [B242] Someone unable to write should dictate it to someone who can, diminishing nothing but dictating as it came to his heart. If his expression is weak, let someone who expresses himself well dictate justly on his behalf, without adding or subtracting meaning. Let him call men skilled in this art—the gnostics—to witness it; failing them, whoever is present among firmly established spiritual poor, lest the wisdom contain a defect arising from its recipient’s deficiency. Or let him call two upright witnesses to the incoming inspiration: the Book and the Sunna. If it accords with them, it is accepted; otherwise it is rejected. Al-Junayd said: “A subtle point may fall into my heart, but I do not accept it without the testimony of two upright witnesses: the Book and the Sunna.” If you are in haste and find no scribe, entrust these inspirations as pledges within one another’s hearts until they can be recorded. Whoever conceals incoming inspirations from his shaykh or brothers has a sinful heart, for that is a kind of betrayal on the path of spiritual nurture. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then warns His servants against opposing His commands.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then warns His servants against opposing His commands."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3709,"arabic_characters":2884},{"id":"q2-284","q":"2:284","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:284 · The reckoning of what is within","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 316–317","cairo":"I:316–317","source_page_first":316,"source_page_last":317,"ar":"لِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَإِنْ تُبْدُوا ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ يُحاسِبْكُمْ بِهِ اللَّهُ فَيَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (284)\nقلت: من قرأ (فيغفر) بالجزم، فعلى العطف على الجواب، ومن قرأ بالرفع فعلى الاستئناف، أي: فهو يغفرُ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ خلقا وملكاً وعبيداً، يتصرف فيهم كيف شاء يرحم من يشاء بفضله، ويعذب من يشاء بعدله، وَإِنْ تُبْدُوا أي: تظهروا ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ من السوء والعزم عليه، أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ في قلوبكم، يُحاسِبْكُمْ بِهِ اللَّهُ يوم القيامة فَيَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ مغفرته، وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ تعذيبه، وَاللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ لا يعجزه عذاب أحد ولا مغفرته. وعبّر الحق تعالى بالمحاسبة دون المؤاخذة، فلم يقل: يؤاخذكم به الله لأن المحاسبة أعم، فتصدق بتقرير الذنوب دون المؤاخذة بها، لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «يدنو المؤمن من ربه حتى يضع كنفه عليه، فيقرره بذنوبه، فيقول: هل تعرف\nكذا؟ فيقول: يا رب، أعرف، فيوقفه على ذنبه ذنباً، ذنباً فيقول الله تعالى: أنا الذي سترتها عليك في الدنيا، وأنا أغفرها لك اليوم» . فلله الفضل والمنّة، وله الحمد والشكر.\nالإشارة: (وإن تبدوا ما في أنفسكم) من الخواطر الردية والطوارق الشيطانية، أو تخفوه في قلوبكم، حتى يحول بينكم وبين شهود محبوبكم، (يحاسبكم به الله فيغفر لمن يشاء) فيمحو ظلمته من قلبه بإلهام التوبة والمبادرة إلى اليقظة، (ويعذب من يشاء) بتركه مع ظلمة تلك الأغيار، وخوضه في بحار تلك الأكدار، فما منع القلوب من مشاهدة الأنوار إلا اشتغالها بظلمة الأغيار، فرّغْ قلبك من الأغيار تملأه بالمعارف والأسرار، فإن أردت أن تكون عين العين، فامح من قلبك نقطة الغين، وهي نقطة السوى، ولله درّ القائل:\nإِنْ تَلاشَى الكونُ عَنْ عَيْنِ كشْفِي ... شاهَدَ السرُّ غَيْبَه في بَيَاني\nفاطْرح الكونَ عن عِيانِكَ وامْحُ ... نقطةَ الغَيْنِ إنْ أردتَ تَرَاني\nواعلم أن الخواطر أربعة: ملكي ورباني ونفساني وشيطاني، فالملكي والرباني لا يأمران إلا بالخير، والنفساني والشيطاني لا يأمران إلا بالشر، وقد يأمران بالخير إذا كان فيه دسيسة إلى الشر، والفرق بين النفساني والشيطاني:\nأن الخاطر النفساني ثابت لا يزول بتعوذ ولا غيره، إلا بسابق العناية، بخلاف الشيطاني: فإنه يزول بذكر الله، ويرجع مع الغفلة عن الله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولمّا نزل قوله تعالى: وَإِنْ تُبْدُوا ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ ... الآية. شق ذلك على الصحابة- رضى الله عنهم- فجاء الصدِّيقُ والفاروق وعبدُ الرحمن ومعاذ، وناسُ من الأنصار، فَجَثَوْا على الرُّكَب، وقالوا: يا رسول الله، ما نزلت علينا آيةٌ أشد من هذه الآية وأنا إن أخذنا بما نُحَدِّثُ به أنفَسنا هَلَكْنَا! فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «هكذا نزلت» . فقالوا: كُلِّفنا من العمل ما لا نطيق، فقال- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «فلعلكم تقولون كما قالت بنو إسرائيل: سَمِعْنا وَعَصَيْنا، قولوا: سمعنا وأطعنا» ، فقالوا: سَمِعْنَا وَأَطَعْنَا، وذَلَّتْ بها أَلْسِنَتُهُمْ، فأَنْزَلَ اللهُ التخفيف، وحكى ما وقع لهم من الإيمان والإذعان، فقال:","quran_ar":"لِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَإِنْ تُبْدُوا ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ يُحاسِبْكُمْ بِهِ اللَّهُ فَيَغْفِرُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (284)","quran_en":"284. To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth. Whether you disclose what is within yourselves or conceal it, God will call you to account for it. He then forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills; and God has power over all things.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Reckoning and its consequence","html":"<p>I say: whoever reads “then He forgives” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فيغفر<\/bdi>) in the jussive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجزم<\/bdi>) coordinates it with the conditional response (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجواب<\/bdi>). Whoever reads it in the indicative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرفع<\/bdi>) takes it as a fresh statement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستئناف<\/bdi>): that is, “He, then, forgives.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: whoever reads “then He forgives” ( فيغفر ) in the jussive ( الجزم ) coordinates it with the conditional response ( الجواب ). Whoever reads it in the indicative ( الرفع ) takes it as a fresh statement ( الاستئناف ): that is, “He, then, forgives.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Being called to account is not necessarily being punished","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth,” as His creation, possession and servants. He disposes of them as He wills, showing mercy to whom He wills by His bounty and punishing whom He wills by His justice. “Whether you disclose”—make manifest—“what is within yourselves” of evil and resolve to commit it, “or conceal it” in your hearts, “God will call you to account for it” on the Day of Resurrection. “He then forgives whom He wills” to forgive “and punishes whom He wills” to punish. “God has power over all things”: neither punishing nor forgiving anyone is beyond His power. The Real, exalted is He, expresses this by “calling to account” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحاسبة<\/bdi>), not by “holding culpable” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المؤاخذة<\/bdi>): He did not say, “God will hold you culpable for it.” Calling to account is more general, and can be fulfilled by making sins acknowledged without exacting punishment for them. For the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “The believer draws near to his Lord until He places His shelter over him and makes him acknowledge his sins, saying, ‘Do you recognize<\/p><p>such-and-such?’ He says, ‘My Lord, I recognize it.’ Thus He confronts him with his sins, one by one; then God, exalted is He, says, ‘I am the One who concealed them for you in the world, and I forgive them for you today.’” To God belong bounty and gracious favour, and to Him belong praise and thanksgiving.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-282\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B243]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth,” as His creation, possession and servants. He disposes of them as He wills, showing mercy to whom He wills by His bounty and punishing whom He wills by His justice. “Whether you disclose”—make manifest—“what is within yourselves” of evil and resolve to commit it, “or conceal it” in your hearts, “God will call you to account for it” on the Day of Resurrection. “He then forgives whom He wills” to forgive “and punishes whom He wills” to punish. “God has power over all things”: neither punishing nor forgiving anyone is beyond His power. The Real, exalted is He, expresses this by “calling to account” ( المحاسبة ), not by “holding culpable” ( المؤاخذة ): He did not say, “God will hold you culpable for it.” Calling to account is more general, and can be fulfilled by making sins acknowledged without exacting punishment for them. For the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “The believer draws near to his Lord until He places His shelter over him and makes him acknowledge his sins, saying, ‘Do you recognize such-and-such?’ He says, ‘My Lord, I recognize it.’ Thus He confronts him with his sins, one by one; then God, exalted is He, says, ‘I am the One who concealed them for you in the world, and I forgive them for you today.’” To God belong bounty and gracious favour, and to Him belong praise and thanksgiving. [B243]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Clearing the heart of otherness","html":"<p>“Whether you disclose what is within yourselves” of evil incoming thoughts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخواطر الردية<\/bdi>) and satanic visitations, or conceal it in your hearts until it interposes between you and witnessing your Beloved, “God will call you to account for it. He then forgives whom He wills”: He effaces its darkness from that person’s heart by inspiring repentance and a swift return to wakefulness. “And He punishes whom He wills” by leaving him with the darkness of those other things, plunging into the seas of those troubles. Nothing prevents hearts from witnessing the lights but their occupation with the darkness of otherness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأغيار<\/bdi>). Empty your heart of otherness, and you will fill it with recognitions and mysteries. To become the very eye of the Eye (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين العين<\/bdi>), efface from your heart the dot of the letter ghayn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغين<\/bdi>): it is the dot of all that is other (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السوى<\/bdi>). How excellent is the poet’s saying:<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-283\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B244]<\/a><\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When the cosmos vanishes from the eye of my unveiling,<br>the inmost secret witnesses its unseen in my disclosure.<br>Cast the cosmos from your direct vision, and erase<br>the dot of ghayn, should you wish to see me.<\/blockquote><p>Know that incoming thoughts are of four kinds: angelic, lordly, from the lower self, and satanic (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ملكي، رباني، نفساني، شيطاني<\/bdi>). The angelic and the lordly command only good; those from the lower self and Satan command only evil. The latter may command good when a concealed means to evil lies within it. The difference between the thought arising from the lower self and the satanic thought is that the former persists: neither seeking refuge nor anything else removes it, except antecedent divine care. The satanic thought, by contrast, departs with remembrance of God and returns with heedlessness of God. And God, exalted is He, knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"“Whether you disclose what is within yourselves” of evil incoming thoughts ( الخواطر الردية ) and satanic visitations, or conceal it in your hearts until it interposes between you and witnessing your Beloved, “God will call you to account for it. He then forgives whom He wills”: He effaces its darkness from that person’s heart by inspiring repentance and a swift return to wakefulness. “And He punishes whom He wills” by leaving him with the darkness of those other things, plunging into the seas of those troubles. Nothing prevents hearts from witnessing the lights but their occupation with the darkness of otherness ( الأغيار ). Empty your heart of otherness, and you will fill it with recognitions and mysteries. To become the very eye of the Eye ( عين العين ), efface from your heart the dot of the letter ghayn ( الغين ): it is the dot of all that is other ( السوى ). How excellent is the poet’s saying: [B244] When the cosmos vanishes from the eye of my unveiling, the inmost secret witnesses its unseen in my disclosure. Cast the cosmos from your direct vision, and erase the dot of ghayn, should you wish to see me. Know that incoming thoughts are of four kinds: angelic, lordly, from the lower self, and satanic ( ملكي، رباني، نفساني، شيطاني ). The angelic and the lordly command only good; those from the lower self and Satan command only evil. The latter may command good when a concealed means to evil lies within it. The difference between the thought arising from the lower self and the satanic thought is that the former persists: neither seeking refuge nor anything else removes it, except antecedent divine care. The satanic thought, by contrast, departs with remembrance of God and returns with heedlessness of God. And God, exalted is He, knows best."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The Companions’ submission and the ensuing relief","html":"<p>When His words, “Whether you disclose what is within yourselves …,” were revealed, they weighed heavily upon the Companions—God be pleased with them. Al-Ṣiddīq, al-Fārūq, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Muʿādh and some of the Anṣār came, sank to their knees and said: “Messenger of God, no verse more burdensome than this has been sent down to us. If we are taken to task for what we say within ourselves, we shall perish!” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, “Thus has it been revealed.” They said, “We have been charged with deeds beyond our power.” He said, blessings and peace be upon him: “Are you, then, perhaps saying what the Children of Israel said, ‘We hear and disobey’? Say, ‘We hear and obey.’” They said, “We hear and obey,” and their tongues yielded to these words. God then sent down relief and related their faith and submission, saying:<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-284\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B245]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When His words, “Whether you disclose what is within yourselves …,” were revealed, they weighed heavily upon the Companions—God be pleased with them. Al-Ṣiddīq, al-Fārūq, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Muʿādh and some of the Anṣār came, sank to their knees and said: “Messenger of God, no verse more burdensome than this has been sent down to us. If we are taken to task for what we say within ourselves, we shall perish!” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, “Thus has it been revealed.” They said, “We have been charged with deeds beyond our power.” He said, blessings and peace be upon him: “Are you, then, perhaps saying what the Children of Israel said, ‘We hear and disobey’? Say, ‘We hear and obey.’” They said, “We hear and obey,” and their tongues yielded to these words. God then sent down relief and related their faith and submission, saying: [B245]"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4338,"arabic_characters":2706},{"id":"q2-285-286","q":"2:285–286","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:285–286 · Faith, capacity and the final supplication","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 317–320","cairo":"I:317–320","source_page_first":317,"source_page_last":320,"ar":"آمَنَ الرَّسُولُ بِما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْهِ مِنْ رَبِّهِ وَالْمُؤْمِنُونَ كُلٌّ آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ وَمَلائِكَتِهِ وَكُتُبِهِ وَرُسُلِهِ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْ رُسُلِهِ وَقالُوا سَمِعْنا وَأَطَعْنا غُفْرانَكَ رَبَّنا وَإِلَيْكَ الْمَصِيرُ (285) لا يُكَلِّفُ اللَّهُ نَفْساً إِلاَّ وُسْعَها لَها مَا كَسَبَتْ وَعَلَيْها مَا اكْتَسَبَتْ رَبَّنا لا تُؤاخِذْنا إِنْ نَسِينا أَوْ أَخْطَأْنا رَبَّنا وَلا تَحْمِلْ عَلَيْنا إِصْراً كَما حَمَلْتَهُ عَلَى الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِنا رَبَّنا وَلا تُحَمِّلْنا ما لا طاقَةَ لَنا بِهِ وَاعْفُ عَنَّا وَاغْفِرْ لَنا وَارْحَمْنا أَنْتَ مَوْلانا فَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (286)\nقلت: من قرأ: (لا نفرق) بالنون، فعلى حذف القول، أي: قالوا: لا نفرق، ومن قرأ بالياء فيرجع إلى الكل، أي: لا يفرق كل واحد منهم بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِّن رُّسُلِهِ، و (بين) : من الظروف النسبية، لا تقع إلا بين شيئين أو أشياء، تقول: جلست بين زيد وعمرو، وبين رجلين، أو رجال، ولا تقول بين زيد فقط، وإنما أضيف هنا إلى أحد لأنه في معنى الجماعة، أي: لا نفرق بين آحاد منهم كقوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «ما أُحلَّت الغنائم لأحدٍ، سُودِ الرؤوس، غيركم» . و (غفرانك) : مفعول مطلق، أي: اغفر لنا غفرانك. أو: نطلب غفرانك، فيكون مفعولاً به.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: آمَنَ الرَّسُولُ بِما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْهِ مِنْ رَبِّهِ إيمان تحقيق وشهود، وَالْمُؤْمِنُونَ كل على قدر إيقان، كُلٌّ واحد منهم آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ على ما يليق به من شهود وعيان، أو دليل وبرهان، وآمن بملائكته وأنهم عباد مكرمون لاَّ يَعْصُونَ اللَّهَ مَآ أَمَرَهُمْ وَيَفْعَلُونَ ما يُؤْمَرُونَ، وَكُتُبِهِ وأنها كلام الله، مشتملة على أمر ونهى ووعد ووعيد وقصص وأخبار، ما عرف منها كالتوراة والإنجيل والزبور والفرقان، وجب الإيمان به بعينه، ومالم يعرف وجب الإيمان به في الجملة، وَرُسُلِهِ وأنهم بشر متصفون بالكمالات، منزّهون عن النقائص، كما يليق بحالهم، حال كون الرسول والمؤمنون قائلين لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْ رُسُلِهِ أو: (لا يفرق) كل منهم بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِّن رُّسُلِهِ بأن يصدقوا بالبعض، دون البعض كما فرقت اليهود والنصارى، وَقالُوا أي المؤمنين سَمِعْنا وَأَطَعْنا أي:\nسمعنا قولك وأطعنا أمرك، نطلب غُفْرانَكَ يا ربنا وَإِلَيْكَ الْمَصِيرُ بالبعث والنشور، وهذا إقرار منهم بالبعث الذي هو من تمام أركان الإيمان.\nفلمّا تحقق إيمانُهم، وتيقن إذعانُهم، خفَّف الله عنهم بقوله: لا يُكَلِّفُ اللَّهُ نَفْساً إِلَّا وُسْعَها أي: إلا ما في طاقتها وتسعه قدرتها. وهذا يدل على عدم وقوع التكليف بالمحال ولا يدل على امتناعه. أما المحال العادي(1) فجائز التكليف به، وأما المحال العقلي(2) فيمتنع، إذ لا يتصور وقوعه، وإذا كلف الله عباده بما يطيقونه، فكل نفس لَها ما كَسَبَتْ من الخير فتوفى أجره على التمام، وَعَلَيْها مَا اكْتَسَبَتْ من الشر، فترى جزاءه، إلا أن يعفو ذو الجلال والإكرام.\nوعبر في جانب الخير بالكسب، وفي جانب الشر بالاكتساب، تعليماً للأدب في نسبة الخير إلى الله، والشر إلى العبد. فتأمله.\nثم قالوا في تمام دعائهم: رَبَّنا لا تُؤاخِذْنا إِنْ نَسِينا أَوْ أَخْطَأْنا، أي: لا تؤاخذنا بما أدى بنا إلى نسيان أو خطأ من تفريط أو قلة مبالاة، وفي الحديث: «إن الله رفعَ عن أمتي الْخَطأَ والنِّسْيَانَ وما حدثتْ به نفسها» .\nويجوز أن يراد نفس الخطأ والنسيان إذ لا تمتنع المؤاخذة بهما عقلاً، فإن الذنوب كالسموم، فكما أن تناول السم يؤدى إلى الهلاك، وإن كان خطأ- فتعاطي الذنوب لا يبعد أن يفضي إلى العقاب، وإن لم يكن عزيمة، لكنه تعالى وعد التجاوز عنه رحمة وفضلاً. ويجوز أن يدعو به الإنسان، استدامة واعتداداً بالنعمة فيه. ويؤيد ذلك مفهوم قوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «رفع عن أمتي الخطأ والنِّسْيَانْ» ، أي: فإن غير هذه الأمة كانوا يؤاخذون به، فدلّ على عدم امتناعه. قاله البيضاوي.\nثم قالوا: رَبَّنا وَلا تَحْمِلْ عَلَيْنا إِصْراً أي: عهداً ثقيلاً يأصر ظهورنا، أي: يثقله، فتعذبنا بتركه وعدم حمله، كَما حَمَلْتَهُ عَلَى الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِنا مثل اليهود في تكليفهم بقتل الأنفس في التوبة، وقطع موضع النجاسة، وغير ذلك من التكاليف الشاقة، رَبَّنا وَلا تُحَمِّلْنا ما لا طاقَةَ لَنا بِهِ من التكاليف التي لا تسعها طاقتنا، وهذا يدل على جواز التكليف بما لا يطاق عادة، وإلا لما سئل التخلص منه، وَاعْفُ عَنَّا أي: امح ذنوبنا، وَاغْفِرْ لَنا أي: استر عيوبنا، وَارْحَمْنا أي: تعطف علينا. اعْفُ عَنَّا الصغائر، وَاغْفِرْ لَنا الكبائر، وَارْحَمْنا عند الشدائد والحسرات، أَنْتَ مَوْلانا أي: سيدنا وناصرنا، فَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ فإن من شأن المولى أن ينصر مواليه على الأعداء.\nقال البيضاوي: (رُوِيَ أنه عليه الصلاة والسلام- لمّا دعا بهذه الدعوات قيل له: فعلتُ) . وعنه عليه الصلاة والسلام: «أُنْزِلَ آيتان من كُنوز الجنة، كتبهما الرحمن بيده قبل أن يخلق الخلق بألفَيْ سنة، من قرأهما بعد العشاء الأخيرة أجْزَأتَاه عن قيام الليل» . وعنه عليه الصلاة والسلام: «من قرأ الأيَتينِ مِنْ آخِر سُورَةِ الْبَقَرةِ في لَيْلَة كَفَتَاهُ» . وهو يَرُدُّ قول من استكره أن يقال سورة البقرة، وقال: ينبغي أن يقال السورة التي يذكر فيها البقرة، كما قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «السورة التي يذكر فيها البقرة فسطاط القرآن فتعلموها فإنَّ تعلُّمها بَرَكَة، وَتَرْكَهَا حَسْرَة، ولن يَسْتَطِيعها البَطَلَةُ. قيل: وما البطلة؟ قال: السحرة(1)» .\nالإشارة: يُفهم من سر الآية أن من شق عليه أمر من الأمور، أو عسرت عليه حاجة، أو نزلت به شدة أو بلية، فليرجع إلى الله، ولينطرح بين يدي مولاه، وليعتقد أن الأمور كلها بيده فإنَّ الله تعالى لا يخليه من معونته ورفده، فيخفف عنه ما نزل به، أو يقويه على حمله، فإن الصحابة- رضي الله عنهم- لما شق عليهم المحاسبة على الخواطر سلَّموا وأذعنوا لأمر مولاهم، فأنزل عليهم التخفيف، وأسقط عنهم في ذلك التكليف، وكل من رجع في أموره كلها إلى الله قضيت حوائجه كلها بالله. «من علامات النجح في النهايات الرجوع إلى الله في البدايات» .\nوقوله تعالى: رَبَّنا وَلا تُحَمِّلْنا ما لا طاقَةَ لَنا بِهِ، قيل: هو الحب لله، فلا يسأل العبد من مولاه من حبه إلا ما يطيقه، وتأمل قضية الرجل الذي سأل سيدنا موسى عليه السلام أن يرزقه الله حبه، فلما سأل ربه موسى عليه السلام هام ذلك الرجل، وشق ثيابه، وتمزقت أوصاله حتى مات. فناجى موسى رضى الله عنه ربه في شأنه، فقال:\nيا موسى، ألف رجل كلهم سألوني ما سأل ذلك الرجل، فقسمت جزءاً من محبتي بينهم، فنابه ذلك الجزء. أو كما قال سبحانه.\nوقال بعض الصالحين: حضرتُ مجلس ذي النون، في فسطاط مصر، فَحَزَرْت(1) في مجلسه سبعين ألفا، فتكلم ذلك اليوم في محبته تعالى فمات أحدَ عشرَ رجلاً في المجلس، فصاح رجل من المريدين فقال: يا أبا الفيض، ذكرْتَ محبة الله تعالى فاذكر محبة المخلوقين، فتأوّه ذو النون تأوّهاً شديداً، ومدّ يده إلى قميصه، وشقه اثنتين، وقال: آه! غلقت رهونهم، واستعبرت عيونهم، وحالفوا السُّهَاد، وفارقوا الرُّقاد، فليلُهم طويل، ونومهم قليل، أحزانهم لا تُنْفذ. وهمومهم لا تفقد، أمورهم عسيرة، ودموعهم غزيرة، باكية عيونهم، قريحة جفونهم، عاداهم الزمان والأهل والجيران.\nقلت: هذه حالة العباد والزهاد، أُولي الجد والاجتهاد، غلب عليهم الخوف المزعِج، أو الشوق المقلق، وأما العارفون الواصلون فقد زال عنهم هذا التعب، وأفضوا إلى الراحة بعد النصب، قد وصلوا إلى مشاهدة الحبيب، ومناجاة القريب، فعبادتهم قلبية، وأعمالهم باطنية، بين فكرة ونظرة، مع العكوف في الحضرة، قد سكن شوقهم وزال قلقهم، قد شربوا ورووا، وسكروا وصحوا، فلا تحركهم الأحوال، ولا تهيجهم الأقوال، بل هم كالجبال الرواسي، نفعنا الله بذكرهم، وجعلنا من حزبهم. آمين.\nقوله تعالى: (واعف عنا) ، قال الورتجبي: أي: (واعف عنا) قلة المعرفة بك، (واغفر لنا) التقصير في عبادتك، (وارحمنا) بمواصلتك ومشاهدتك. هـ. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق، وسلام على المرسلين، والحمد لله رب العالمين.","quran_ar":"آمَنَ الرَّسُولُ بِما أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْهِ مِنْ رَبِّهِ وَالْمُؤْمِنُونَ كُلٌّ آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ وَمَلائِكَتِهِ وَكُتُبِهِ وَرُسُلِهِ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْ رُسُلِهِ وَقالُوا سَمِعْنا وَأَطَعْنا غُفْرانَكَ رَبَّنا وَإِلَيْكَ الْمَصِيرُ (285) لا يُكَلِّفُ اللَّهُ نَفْساً إِلاَّ وُسْعَها لَها مَا كَسَبَتْ وَعَلَيْها مَا اكْتَسَبَتْ رَبَّنا لا تُؤاخِذْنا إِنْ نَسِينا أَوْ أَخْطَأْنا رَبَّنا وَلا تَحْمِلْ عَلَيْنا إِصْراً كَما حَمَلْتَهُ عَلَى الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِنا رَبَّنا وَلا تُحَمِّلْنا ما لا طاقَةَ لَنا بِهِ وَاعْفُ عَنَّا وَاغْفِرْ لَنا وَارْحَمْنا أَنْتَ مَوْلانا فَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (286)","quran_en":"285. The Messenger believes in what has been sent down to him from his Lord, and so do the believers. Each believes in God, His angels, His books and His messengers: “We make no distinction between any of His messengers.” They say, “We hear and obey. Your forgiveness, our Lord! To You is the return.”\n286. God charges no soul beyond its capacity. To its credit is what it has earned, and against it is what it has acquired. “Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err. Our Lord, do not lay upon us a burden such as You laid upon those before us. Our Lord, do not make us bear what we have no strength to bear. Pardon us, forgive us and have mercy on us. You are our Master: give us victory over the unbelieving people.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Collective reference and the plea for forgiveness","html":"<p>I say: in the reading “we make no distinction” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا نفرق<\/bdi>), with nūn, a verb of saying is understood: “They say, ‘We make no distinction.’” In the reading with yāʾ, the reference is to “each”: each one of them makes no distinction “between any of His messengers.” “Between” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بين<\/bdi>) is a relational adverb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الظروف النسبية<\/bdi>), occurring only between two or more things. You say, “I sat between Zayd and ʿAmr,” “between two men,” or “between men,” but not “between Zayd” alone. Here it is annexed to “any one” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أحد<\/bdi>) because that word has a collective meaning: “We make no distinction between individuals among them.” Compare the Prophet’s saying, blessings and peace be upon him, “Spoils were never made lawful to anyone—black-haired heads—other than you.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-285\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B246]<\/a> “Your forgiveness” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غفرانك<\/bdi>) is an absolute accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول مطلق<\/bdi>): “Forgive us with Your forgiveness.” Alternatively, “[We seek] Your forgiveness,” in which case it is a direct object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول به<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: in the reading “we make no distinction” ( لا نفرق ), with nūn, a verb of saying is understood: “They say, ‘We make no distinction.’” In the reading with yāʾ, the reference is to “each”: each one of them makes no distinction “between any of His messengers.” “Between” ( بين ) is a relational adverb ( الظروف النسبية ), occurring only between two or more things. You say, “I sat between Zayd and ʿAmr,” “between two men,” or “between men,” but not “between Zayd” alone. Here it is annexed to “any one” ( أحد ) because that word has a collective meaning: “We make no distinction between individuals among them.” Compare the Prophet’s saying, blessings and peace be upon him, “Spoils were never made lawful to anyone—black-haired heads—other than you.” [B246] “Your forgiveness” ( غفرانك ) is an absolute accusative ( مفعول مطلق ): “Forgive us with Your forgiveness.” Alternatively, “[We seek] Your forgiveness,” in which case it is a direct object ( مفعول به )."},{"type":"theology","title":"The degrees and objects of faith","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “The Messenger believes in what has been sent down to him from his Lord,” with the faith of realization and witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تحقيق وشهود<\/bdi>); “and the believers,” each according to his certainty. “Each” one of them “believes in God” as befits him: through witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود وعيان<\/bdi>), or evidence and demonstration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دليل وبرهان<\/bdi>). Each believes in His angels: that they are honoured servants, who “do not disobey God in what He commands them, and do what they are commanded” [66:6]. And in “His books”: that they are God’s speech, containing command and prohibition, promise and warning, narratives and reports. Those known by name—the Torah, Gospel, Psalms and Criterion—must be believed in individually; those not known must be believed in collectively. And in “His messengers”: that they are human beings endowed with perfections and free from defects in a manner appropriate to their condition. The Messenger and believers say, “We make no distinction between any of His messengers”; or, in the other reading, each one “makes no distinction between any of His messengers.” They do not affirm some and deny others, as the Jews and Christians made distinctions. “They say”—the believers—“We hear and obey”: we hear Your word and obey Your command. We seek “Your forgiveness, our Lord! To You is the return,” through resurrection and rising from the dead. This is their acknowledgement of resurrection, which completes the pillars of faith.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “The Messenger believes in what has been sent down to him from his Lord,” with the faith of realization and witnessing ( تحقيق وشهود ); “and the believers,” each according to his certainty. “Each” one of them “believes in God” as befits him: through witnessing and direct vision ( شهود وعيان ), or evidence and demonstration ( دليل وبرهان ). Each believes in His angels: that they are honoured servants, who “do not disobey God in what He commands them, and do what they are commanded” [66:6]. And in “His books”: that they are God’s speech, containing command and prohibition, promise and warning, narratives and reports. Those known by name—the Torah, Gospel, Psalms and Criterion—must be believed in individually; those not known must be believed in collectively. And in “His messengers”: that they are human beings endowed with perfections and free from defects in a manner appropriate to their condition. The Messenger and believers say, “We make no distinction between any of His messengers”; or, in the other reading, each one “makes no distinction between any of His messengers.” They do not affirm some and deny others, as the Jews and Christians made distinctions. “They say”—the believers—“We hear and obey”: we hear Your word and obey Your command. We seek “Your forgiveness, our Lord! To You is the return,” through resurrection and rising from the dead. This is their acknowledgement of resurrection, which completes the pillars of faith."},{"type":"theology","title":"Capacity, acquisition and divine pardon","html":"<p>When their faith was realized and their submission established, God lightened their burden by saying, “God charges no soul beyond its capacity”: only what lies within its strength and the extent of its ability. This indicates that imposing the impossible does not occur; it does not establish that such imposition is itself impossible. As for what is impossible according to ordinary custom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحال العادي<\/bdi>), imposing it is admissible; what is rationally impossible (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحال العقلي<\/bdi>) cannot be imposed, since its occurrence is inconceivable.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-286\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B247]<\/a> When God charges His servants with what they can bear, each soul has “to its credit what it has earned” of good, and is paid its reward in full; “and against it what it has acquired” of evil, whose recompense it will see, unless the Possessor of Majesty and Generosity pardons it. On the side of good He uses “earning” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكسب<\/bdi>), and on the side of evil “acquiring” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاكتساب<\/bdi>), teaching reverent propriety (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>) in attributing good to God and evil to the servant. So consider this. They then complete their supplication: “Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err.” That is, do not take us to task for the negligence or lack of concern that has led us into forgetfulness or error. The report says: “God has lifted from my community error, forgetfulness and what it says within itself.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-287\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B248]<\/a><\/p><p>Alternatively, the intended meaning is error and forgetfulness themselves. Punishment for these is not rationally impossible. Sins resemble poisons: just as taking poison leads to destruction even when it is taken in error, so committing sins might conceivably lead to punishment even without deliberate resolve. But He, exalted is He, has promised to overlook them in mercy and bounty. A person may continue to pray for this in order that the favour endure, and in acknowledgement of it. This is supported by the implication of the Prophet’s words, “Error and forgetfulness have been lifted from my community”: those outside this community were taken to task for them, which shows that such punishment is not impossible. So says al-Bayḍāwī. They then say, “Our Lord, do not lay upon us a burden”: a heavy covenant that weighs upon our backs, so that You punish us for abandoning it and failing to carry it; “such as You laid upon those before us.” An example is the Jews’ being required to kill themselves in repentance, cut away the place affected by impurity, and undertake other arduous obligations. “Our Lord, do not make us bear what we have no strength to bear”: obligations exceeding our capacity. This indicates the admissibility of imposing what is ordinarily beyond one’s capacity; otherwise, deliverance from it would not be requested. “Pardon us”: efface our sins. “Forgive us”: veil our faults. “Have mercy on us”: turn tenderly towards us. Or: pardon our lesser sins, forgive our greater sins, and have mercy upon us in hardships and regrets. “You are our Master,” our Lord and Helper: “give us victory over the unbelieving people,” for it is the master’s part to help those attached to him against their enemies.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When their faith was realized and their submission established, God lightened their burden by saying, “God charges no soul beyond its capacity”: only what lies within its strength and the extent of its ability. This indicates that imposing the impossible does not occur; it does not establish that such imposition is itself impossible. As for what is impossible according to ordinary custom ( المحال العادي ), imposing it is admissible; what is rationally impossible ( المحال العقلي ) cannot be imposed, since its occurrence is inconceivable. [B247] When God charges His servants with what they can bear, each soul has “to its credit what it has earned” of good, and is paid its reward in full; “and against it what it has acquired” of evil, whose recompense it will see, unless the Possessor of Majesty and Generosity pardons it. On the side of good He uses “earning” ( الكسب ), and on the side of evil “acquiring” ( الاكتساب ), teaching reverent propriety ( الأدب ) in attributing good to God and evil to the servant. So consider this. They then complete their supplication: “Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err.” That is, do not take us to task for the negligence or lack of concern that has led us into forgetfulness or error. The report says: “God has lifted from my community error, forgetfulness and what it says within itself.” [B248] Alternatively, the intended meaning is error and forgetfulness themselves. Punishment for these is not rationally impossible. Sins resemble poisons: just as taking poison leads to destruction even when it is taken in error, so committing sins might conceivably lead to punishment even without deliberate resolve. But He, exalted is He, has promised to overlook them in mercy and bounty. A person may continue to pray for this in order that the favour endure, and in acknowledgement of it. This is supported by the implication of the Prophet’s words, “Error and forgetfulness have been lifted from my community”: those outside this community were taken to task for them, which shows that such punishment is not impossible. So says al-Bayḍāwī. They then say, “Our Lord, do not lay upon us a burden”: a heavy covenant that weighs upon our backs, so that You punish us for abandoning it and failing to carry it; “such as You laid upon those before us.” An example is the Jews’ being required to kill themselves in repentance, cut away the place affected by impurity, and undertake other arduous obligations. “Our Lord, do not make us bear what we have no strength to bear”: obligations exceeding our capacity. This indicates the admissibility of imposing what is ordinarily beyond one’s capacity; otherwise, deliverance from it would not be requested. “Pardon us”: efface our sins. “Forgive us”: veil our faults. “Have mercy on us”: turn tenderly towards us. Or: pardon our lesser sins, forgive our greater sins, and have mercy upon us in hardships and regrets. “You are our Master,” our Lord and Helper: “give us victory over the unbelieving people,” for it is the master’s part to help those attached to him against their enemies."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The closing verses and the merit of al-Baqara","html":"<p>Al-Bayḍāwī says: “It is related that when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, made these supplications, he was told, ‘I have done so.’” It is also related from him: “Two verses were sent down from the treasures of Paradise, written by the All-Merciful with His hand two thousand years before He created creation. Whoever recites them after the final evening prayer, they suffice him in place of standing in prayer through the night.” And from him: “Whoever recites the two verses at the end of Sūrat al-Baqara in a night, they suffice him.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-288\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B249]<\/a> The latter report refutes the view of one who disliked saying “Sūrat al-Baqara,” maintaining that one should say, “The sūrah in which the cow is mentioned.” Compare his words, blessings and peace be upon him: “The sūrah in which the cow is mentioned is the pavilion of the Qurʾān. Learn it: learning it is a blessing, abandoning it a cause of regret, and the baṭala cannot withstand it.” When asked who the baṭala were, he answered, “Sorcerers.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-289\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B250]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Bayḍāwī says: “It is related that when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, made these supplications, he was told, ‘I have done so.’” It is also related from him: “Two verses were sent down from the treasures of Paradise, written by the All-Merciful with His hand two thousand years before He created creation. Whoever recites them after the final evening prayer, they suffice him in place of standing in prayer through the night.” And from him: “Whoever recites the two verses at the end of Sūrat al-Baqara in a night, they suffice him.” [B249] The latter report refutes the view of one who disliked saying “Sūrat al-Baqara,” maintaining that one should say, “The sūrah in which the cow is mentioned.” Compare his words, blessings and peace be upon him: “The sūrah in which the cow is mentioned is the pavilion of the Qurʾān. Learn it: learning it is a blessing, abandoning it a cause of regret, and the baṭala cannot withstand it.” When asked who the baṭala were, he answered, “Sorcerers.” [B250]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Returning to God in difficulty","html":"<p>From the verse’s inward meaning it is understood that anyone for whom a matter becomes difficult, a need hard to obtain, or upon whom hardship or affliction descends, should return to God, cast himself down before his Master and believe that all affairs are in His hand. God, exalted is He, will not leave him without assistance and support: He will lighten what has befallen him, or strengthen him to bear it. When the Companions—God be pleased with them—found reckoning for incoming thoughts burdensome, they submitted and yielded to their Master’s command. He then sent relief upon them and removed the obligation in that respect. Whoever returns all his affairs to God has all his needs fulfilled through God. “Among the signs of success at the ends is returning to God at the beginnings.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-290\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B251]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"From the verse’s inward meaning it is understood that anyone for whom a matter becomes difficult, a need hard to obtain, or upon whom hardship or affliction descends, should return to God, cast himself down before his Master and believe that all affairs are in His hand. God, exalted is He, will not leave him without assistance and support: He will lighten what has befallen him, or strengthen him to bear it. When the Companions—God be pleased with them—found reckoning for incoming thoughts burdensome, they submitted and yielded to their Master’s command. He then sent relief upon them and removed the obligation in that respect. Whoever returns all his affairs to God has all his needs fulfilled through God. “Among the signs of success at the ends is returning to God at the beginnings.” [B251]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Love, overwhelming states and settled realization","html":"<p>Regarding His words, “Our Lord, do not make us bear what we have no strength to bear,” it has been said that what is meant is love for God. A servant should therefore ask his Master for no more of His love than he can bear. Consider the story of the man who asked our master Moses, peace be upon him, to ask God to bestow His love upon him. When Moses asked his Lord, the man wandered distraught, rent his clothes, and his limbs were torn apart until he died. Moses privately addressed his Lord concerning him, and He said: “Moses, a thousand men all asked Me what this man asked. I divided a portion of My love among them, and that share fell to him”—or words to that effect from Him, exalted is He.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-291\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B252]<\/a> One of the righteous said: “I attended Dhū l-Nūn’s gathering in Fusṭāṭ, Egypt. I estimated those present at seventy thousand. That day he spoke of love of God, and eleven men died in the gathering. A disciple cried, ‘Abū l-Fayḍ, you have spoken of love of God; speak now of the love of created beings.’<a class=\"note-ref\" href=\"#/note/XV-N004\">[XV-N004]<\/a> Dhū l-Nūn gave a deep groan, reached towards his shirt, tore it in two, and said: ‘Ah! Their pledges are forfeit, their eyes overflow. They have allied themselves with sleeplessness and parted company with slumber. Their nights are long, their sleep little. Their sorrows do not run out, their cares do not depart. Their affairs are hard, their tears abundant, their eyes weeping, their eyelids sore. Time, family and neighbours have become their enemies.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-292\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B252]<\/a> I say: this is the condition of worshippers and ascetics, people of strenuous effort and exertion, overcome by disturbing fear or unsettling longing. As for the gnostics who have arrived (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارفون الواصلون<\/bdi>), this weariness has left them, and after toil they have passed into repose. They have reached the witnessing of the Beloved and intimate converse with the Near. Their worship is of the heart, their works inward: now contemplation, now vision, while abiding in the Presence. Their longing has grown still and their disquiet departed. They have drunk and been quenched, become intoxicated and returned to sobriety. States no longer move them, nor do words excite them; rather, they are like firmly rooted mountains. May God benefit us through their remembrance and make us of their company. Amen. On His words, “Pardon us,” al-Wartajbī says: “‘Pardon us’ our slight knowledge of You; ‘forgive us’ our shortcoming in worshipping You; and ‘have mercy on us’ through communion with You and witnessing You.” And success is through God. He guides to the even path. Peace be upon the messengers, and praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Regarding His words, “Our Lord, do not make us bear what we have no strength to bear,” it has been said that what is meant is love for God. A servant should therefore ask his Master for no more of His love than he can bear. Consider the story of the man who asked our master Moses, peace be upon him, to ask God to bestow His love upon him. When Moses asked his Lord, the man wandered distraught, rent his clothes, and his limbs were torn apart until he died. Moses privately addressed his Lord concerning him, and He said: “Moses, a thousand men all asked Me what this man asked. I divided a portion of My love among them, and that share fell to him”—or words to that effect from Him, exalted is He. [B252] One of the righteous said: “I attended Dhū l-Nūn’s gathering in Fusṭāṭ, Egypt. I estimated those present at seventy thousand. That day he spoke of love of God, and eleven men died in the gathering. A disciple cried, ‘Abū l-Fayḍ, you have spoken of love of God; speak now of the love of created beings.’ [XV-N004] Dhū l-Nūn gave a deep groan, reached towards his shirt, tore it in two, and said: ‘Ah! Their pledges are forfeit, their eyes overflow. They have allied themselves with sleeplessness and parted company with slumber. Their nights are long, their sleep little. Their sorrows do not run out, their cares do not depart. Their affairs are hard, their tears abundant, their eyes weeping, their eyelids sore. Time, family and neighbours have become their enemies.’” [B252] I say: this is the condition of worshippers and ascetics, people of strenuous effort and exertion, overcome by disturbing fear or unsettling longing. As for the gnostics who have arrived ( العارفون الواصلون ), this weariness has left them, and after toil they have passed into repose. They have reached the witnessing of the Beloved and intimate converse with the Near. Their worship is of the heart, their works inward: now contemplation, now vision, while abiding in the Presence. Their longing has grown still and their disquiet departed. They have drunk and been quenched, become intoxicated and returned to sobriety. States no longer move them, nor do words excite them; rather, they are like firmly rooted mountains. May God benefit us through their remembrance and make us of their company. Amen. On His words, “Pardon us,” al-Wartajbī says: “‘Pardon us’ our slight knowledge of You; ‘forgive us’ our shortcoming in worshipping You; and ‘have mercy on us’ through communion with You and witnessing You.” And success is through God. He guides to the even path. Peace be upon the messengers, and praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C318.1","page":318,"ar":"المحال العادي: كرفع إنسان جبلا.","en":"An impossibility according to the customary course: for example, a human lifting a mountain.","anchor":"المحال العادي"},{"id":"C318.2","page":318,"ar":"المحال العقلي: كالجمع بين الضدين.","en":"A rational impossibility: for example, combining two opposites.","anchor":"المحال العقلي"},{"id":"C319.1","page":319,"ar":"قال الشهاب الخفاجي في حاشيته على البيضاوي، موفقا بين القائلين بكراهة أن يقال: سورة البقرة، وقول الجمهور بجوازه: إنما المنع من ذلك كان في صدر الإسلام، لما استهزأ سفهاء المشركين بسورة العنكبوت ونحوها، فمنع ذلك دفعا للمعاندين. ثم لما استقر الدين، وقطع الله دابر القوم الظالمين، شاع ذلك وساغ.","en":"Al-Shihāb al-Khafājī, in his supercommentary on al-Bayḍāwī, reconciles those who deem saying “Sūrat al-Baqara” objectionable with the majority who permit it: the prohibition applied only in the early period of Islam, when foolish polytheists mocked Sūrat al-ʿAnkabūt and similar names. The usage was prohibited to counter the obstinate opponents. Then, when religion became established and God cut off the wrongdoing people, the usage became widespread and permissible.","anchor":"السحرة"},{"id":"C320.1","page":320,"ar":"حزر الشيء، حزرا: قدره بالتخمين فهو حازر.","en":"Ḥazara a thing, with the verbal noun ḥazr: he estimated it by conjecture; the person doing so is a ḥāzir.","anchor":"فَحَزَرْت"}],"english_characters":9985,"arabic_characters":6771}];
